WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.395 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:03.832 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.832 --> 00:00:06.227 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.227 --> 00:00:09.215 align:middle line:90%


00:00:09.215 --> 00:00:11.340 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: To participate
in this next exercise,

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.320 align:middle line:84%
you will have to grab
one of these index cards

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.390 align:middle line:84%
that are on the
table that correspond

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:18.600 align:middle line:90%
to a particular technology.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.780 align:middle line:84%
It looks like film,
radio, and television

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.770 align:middle line:84%
are amongst the remaining ones,
and you'll get in small groups

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:26.610 align:middle line:90%
and work on those.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.160 align:middle line:90%
Here's what I'd like you to do.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.860 align:middle line:84%
Find the other people who
have the same card as you.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.030 align:middle line:84%
Discuss what you learned
from the readings, anything

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.510 align:middle line:84%
that stands out to you,
comes in your mind.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.260 align:middle line:84%
But then I want you
to make a chart, which

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.710 align:middle line:84%
you can do on the whiteboard
or on our blue pieces of paper

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.310 align:middle line:84%
there, that capture
three key ideas.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.730 align:middle line:84%
First of all, who are
all of the stakeholders

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:55.040 align:middle line:84%
who are involved in
the implementation

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:55.980 align:middle line:90%
of this technology?

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.460 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that I think
Larry Cuban is particularly

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.080 align:middle line:84%
wonderful at is
giving you a sense

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.160 align:middle line:84%
of all of the different hands
that touch a technology making

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.410 align:middle line:84%
its way into a school, and
all of the different hands

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.430 align:middle line:84%
that touch a technology
when it's in the school.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:13.500 align:middle line:84%
This is incredibly important for
understanding how technologies

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.660 align:middle line:90%
actually get used.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.525 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of times
in canonical design,

00:01:18.525 --> 00:01:20.400 align:middle line:84%
I think this comes up
in software engineering

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.870 align:middle line:84%
classes and things
like this idea

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.930 align:middle line:84%
that there's a
programmer and a user,

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.160 align:middle line:84%
and the user is one
person who's sitting

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.160 align:middle line:84%
at a keyboard with their
fingers on the keyboard

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.535 align:middle line:84%
and like, that's the person
you need to pay attention to.

00:01:31.535 --> 00:01:35.130 align:middle line:84%
And that two part model
of designer and user

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.470 align:middle line:90%
totally breaks down in schools.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.720 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of
people who are very

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.450 align:middle line:84%
different who could be
considered users of technology

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.220 align:middle line:90%
in schools.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.650 align:middle line:84%
Another thing that
Larry Cuban does really

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:50.730 align:middle line:84%
well is he helps
us pay attention

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.800 align:middle line:84%
to how these people have what
I'll call different interests.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.870 align:middle line:84%
By interests, I don't
mean curiosities.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:00.000 align:middle line:84%
I mean incentives, stakes,
how a political scientist

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.410 align:middle line:90%
might use the word interest.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.270 align:middle line:84%
All of these
different stakeholders

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.990 align:middle line:84%
are rewarded for
different kinds of things.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.630 align:middle line:84%
They also have what we'll call
interests or disincentives.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.470 align:middle line:84%
They are also trying to
avoid or will be punished

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.240 align:middle line:90%
for different kinds of things.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.245 align:middle line:84%
So for the technology that you
picked a card of and are talking

00:02:18.245 --> 00:02:19.620 align:middle line:84%
with your colleagues
about, think

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.037 align:middle line:84%
of all of the
different people who

00:02:21.037 --> 00:02:22.980 align:middle line:84%
you think could be
considered stakeholders

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.313 align:middle line:84%
in the implementation
of this thing.

00:02:25.313 --> 00:02:26.980 align:middle line:84%
And then for each of
those stakeholders,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.040 align:middle line:84%
come up with a couple
of their interests

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.800 align:middle line:84%
and a couple of their
interests or disincentives

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.970 align:middle line:84%
or things that punish them
or those kinds of things.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.363 align:middle line:84%
Does that, as a general
exercise, make sense?

00:02:36.363 --> 00:02:38.280 align:middle line:84%
And the thing that we're
trying to do together

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.270 align:middle line:84%
is we want to try to
recreate the world

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.900 align:middle line:90%
around these technologies.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.890 align:middle line:84%
That's what I think Larry Cuban
is so fabulous at is saying,

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.950 align:middle line:84%
yep, a television set is
going to roll into a--

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.230 align:middle line:84%
it's going to roll into a
classroom and into a school

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.313 align:middle line:84%
and into a community, and
there's all these people

00:02:54.313 --> 00:02:55.260 align:middle line:90%
who are part of that.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.760 align:middle line:84%
That's what we really
want to get at here.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.885 align:middle line:84%
The last thing I'll tell
you before I cut you loose

00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:01.620 align:middle line:84%
is Larry Cuban has a totally
fascinating history himself.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.692 align:middle line:84%
He was a social studies teacher
in the '50s and '60s, then he

00:03:04.692 --> 00:03:06.900 align:middle line:84%
was a school principal, then
he was a superintendent.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:07.880 align:middle line:84%
He was actually
the superintendent

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.800 align:middle line:84%
in Arlington, Virginia, which
is where my wife went to school.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.940 align:middle line:84%
So when my wife was an
elementary school student,

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.450 align:middle line:84%
her superintendent at the time
was Larry Cuban, an Arlington,

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.740 align:middle line:90%
Virginia resident--

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.240 align:middle line:90%
Fairfax.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:20.850 align:middle line:90%
Close enough.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.347 align:middle line:84%
And it was only after he had
a pretty distinguished career

00:03:25.347 --> 00:03:27.180 align:middle line:84%
as a teacher, a principal,
a superintendent,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.590 align:middle line:84%
that he went to Stanford,
got his dissertation,

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:33.260 align:middle line:84%
became a historian of
education, and then

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.750 align:middle line:84%
eventually a history of
education professor out there.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.663 align:middle line:84%
So he's someone who's
extremely well-read,

00:03:38.663 --> 00:03:40.830 align:middle line:84%
as I'm sure you got a sense
of and things like that.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.380 align:middle line:84%
But he also just
deeply understands

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.510 align:middle line:84%
the kinds of things that
make schools tick and make

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.050 align:middle line:90%
the people inside schools tick.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:47.850 align:middle line:90%
Does this exercise make sense?

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.260 align:middle line:90%
Find the people with your cards.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.280 align:middle line:90%
Move yourselves around.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.680 align:middle line:84%
Make this little chart,
either on the board

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.070 align:middle line:84%
or on these blue
pieces of paper.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:53.570 align:middle line:90%
Ready?

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.340 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.950 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.150 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Those things
are hugely important.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.900 align:middle line:84%
Like, where does
teaching actually happen?

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.340 align:middle line:84%
There's some human being who
needs to go to the bathroom

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:08.072 align:middle line:90%
and needs to eat.

00:04:08.072 --> 00:04:09.780 align:middle line:84%
I mean, he has that
great quote in there.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.000 align:middle line:84%
It's not about film,
but television,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.500 align:middle line:84%
that they found
that they're more

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.100 align:middle line:84%
likely to use television in the
afternoon than in the mornings.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.829 align:middle line:84%
And it's all like
biology and culture,

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:20.940 align:middle line:84%
our brains work
better in the morning.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.100 align:middle line:84%
We're all a little
flagging by the afternoon,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.390 align:middle line:90%
those kinds of things.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:28.070 align:middle line:84%
And so yeah, I think anybody
who's building a technology

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:32.810 align:middle line:84%
has to think to
themselves, what are--

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:33.870 align:middle line:90%
yeah, yeah.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.315 align:middle line:84%
What are the variations
in a teacher's day?

00:04:36.315 --> 00:04:38.190 align:middle line:84%
When do they have more
energy or less energy?

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.857 align:middle line:84%
How does it fit into those kinds
of cycles and things like that?

00:04:40.857 --> 00:04:44.930 align:middle line:84%
Those are every bit as real
as the test score goals

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.650 align:middle line:90%
that we have or anything else.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.395 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:04:48.395 --> 00:04:55.143 align:middle line:90%


00:04:55.143 --> 00:04:56.560 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: This
is really good.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.393 align:middle line:90%
This is really good.

00:04:57.393 --> 00:04:58.510 align:middle line:90%
Take a few more minutes.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.765 align:middle line:90%
Take a few more minutes.

00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:03.398 align:middle line:90%


00:05:03.398 --> 00:05:05.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We're trying to think
about disinterest for schools

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:06.980 align:middle line:90%
and for producers.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:08.103 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Sure.

00:05:08.103 --> 00:05:10.020 align:middle line:84%
So if you're a school,
what would make you go,

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.300 align:middle line:84%
eh, I'm not sure I want
to do anything with films.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.460 align:middle line:90%
So it's 1913.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.498 align:middle line:84%
Silent films started
coming out in earnest

00:05:17.498 --> 00:05:18.790 align:middle line:90%
around the turn of the century.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:21.300 align:middle line:90%
There's a handful of people.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.920 align:middle line:84%
The most prominent of
them is Thomas Edison.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.030 align:middle line:84%
Guy invents the light bulb and
he gets in front of Congress

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.260 align:middle line:90%
and he says--

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.130 align:middle line:90%
first in 1913, he says--

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.370 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I'll read the other
quote, but he says, in a decade,

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:36.240 align:middle line:84%
textbooks will be
replaced by filmstrips.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.970 align:middle line:84%
That's how powerful this new
technology is going to be.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.860 align:middle line:84%
If you're a school
principal, what

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.172 align:middle line:84%
would be some things
that make you go,

00:05:43.172 --> 00:05:44.880 align:middle line:84%
they're pretty smart,
Thomas, but I'm not

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.712 align:middle line:90%
so sure about this one.

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:49.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe if they just put
a bunch of money into textbooks.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:50.212 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Sure.

00:05:50.212 --> 00:05:52.740 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, they have an
entire infrastructure

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.300 align:middle line:84%
built around the
existing technologies.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.010 align:middle line:90%
They have shelves on the wall.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:58.780 align:middle line:84%
They have libraries
full of books.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.780 align:middle line:84%
Every one of their
teachers has been

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.210 align:middle line:84%
trained to teach with
textbooks, and not

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.120 align:middle line:84%
a single one of their teachers,
when these things are first

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.220 align:middle line:84%
introduced, has
ever been in a class

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.270 align:middle line:84%
as a student
learning from a film.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:13.900 align:middle line:84%
So you have this huge inherent
conservatism in the system.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:14.890 align:middle line:90%
So that could be one.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.400 align:middle line:84%
What would be what
would be some others?

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.530 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.020 align:middle line:90%


00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:24.645 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:06:24.645 --> 00:06:26.603 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I guess it's kind
of adding on to that,

00:06:26.603 --> 00:06:29.560 align:middle line:84%
but integrating it into
a classroom setting.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:34.590 align:middle line:84%
How would they even integrate it
to-- even if they're completely

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.232 align:middle line:84%
on board, how would they even
integrate it into a classroom?

00:06:38.232 --> 00:06:39.690 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: For
films, what would

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.436 align:middle line:84%
be some of the challenges
of integration?

00:06:41.436 --> 00:06:45.095 align:middle line:90%


00:06:45.095 --> 00:06:46.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The
technology that you

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.920 align:middle line:84%
would need to put
into a classroom

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.383 align:middle line:90%
or to move into a school.

00:06:52.383 --> 00:06:53.800 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Tell
me specifically

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.460 align:middle line:84%
for films what some of
that technology might be.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:57.710 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I mean, this isn't--

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:00.910 align:middle line:84%
I don't think the
projectors back then

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.587 align:middle line:90%
could fit into a classroom.

00:07:02.587 --> 00:07:03.920 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, so they could.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.050 align:middle line:90%
I mean, they're portable enough.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:06.920 align:middle line:90%
But they're big.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.430 align:middle line:84%
They're these big lumbering
machines for which the rooms

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:12.760 align:middle line:84%
are-- if you have a classroom,
let's say you have a big

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:16.330 align:middle line:84%
population growth
and so you built--

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:19.990 align:middle line:84%
someone in the 1850s built a
school and it was 12 kids per

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.360 align:middle line:84%
classroom, and now you're
putting 18 kids per classroom

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:23.000 align:middle line:90%
in there.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.790 align:middle line:84%
Like, a totally
legitimate reason.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.355 align:middle line:84%
There is not space in this room
to physically move this machine.

00:07:28.355 --> 00:07:29.980 align:middle line:84%
What are some other
sort of-- what else

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:31.780 align:middle line:84%
do you need for
films as a technology

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.260 align:middle line:90%
to be able to be implemented?

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.623 align:middle line:90%
So you need a giant projector.

00:07:34.623 --> 00:07:36.290 align:middle line:84%
You're going to put
the thing on wheels.

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:37.207 align:middle line:90%
What else do you need?

00:07:37.207 --> 00:07:39.353 align:middle line:90%


00:07:39.353 --> 00:07:41.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A place to
actually project it onto.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:41.780 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK, good.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:43.610 align:middle line:84%
You're going to have to go
to all of your classrooms,

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.220 align:middle line:84%
and you're going to
have to install screens

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:46.262 align:middle line:90%
at the front of the room.

00:07:46.262 --> 00:07:49.840 align:middle line:84%
Not the hugest challenge,
but also not free.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:53.550 align:middle line:84%
Like, so who's going
to do these things?

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:57.320 align:middle line:84%
Films also came on
these huge reels.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:01.080 align:middle line:84%
It wasn't just like pick your
YouTube channel and play.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.600 align:middle line:84%
Where in your
building are you going

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:04.680 align:middle line:90%
to keep a library of film reels?

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.470 align:middle line:84%
Who is going to organize
those libraries of film reels?

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.120 align:middle line:84%
Like if you have four
elementary schools--

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.010 align:middle line:84%
or four elementary
classrooms, let's

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:14.750 align:middle line:84%
say you have four
first grades, are

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:16.582 align:middle line:84%
you going to buy four
copies of this film

00:08:16.582 --> 00:08:18.540 align:middle line:84%
so that everyone can
watch it at the same time?

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.190 align:middle line:84%
If not, how are you going to
physically organize and move

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:25.160 align:middle line:84%
this giant reel from
one space to another?

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.780 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine the principal
being like, but how are we?

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:28.280 align:middle line:90%
But how are?

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:28.940 align:middle line:90%
But how are?

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:29.610 align:middle line:90%
But how are we?

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.610 align:middle line:90%
And all this stuff costs money.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:35.800 align:middle line:90%
So those are all kinds of--

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:37.515 align:middle line:84%
I mean, Thomas Edison
is here telling you

00:08:37.515 --> 00:08:39.140 align:middle line:84%
that you're going to
get 100% efficient

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:40.557 align:middle line:84%
learning through
this thing, which

00:08:40.557 --> 00:08:42.640 align:middle line:84%
is the potential
gains, which no one has

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.640 align:middle line:84%
any evidence for because
it hasn't happened yet.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.160 align:middle line:84%
But you've got to
think to yourself,

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:47.490 align:middle line:90%
what are all these costs?

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.010 align:middle line:84%
What are all these
material changes?

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:50.860 align:middle line:84%
So think about how you can
articulate some of those things.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:51.360 align:middle line:90%
But good.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.560 align:middle line:90%
When you really get into--

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.960 align:middle line:84%
to do this exercise
well, you have to, like,

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.390 align:middle line:90%
what exactly is this technology?

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.227 align:middle line:84%
Even physically,
what does it require?

00:08:59.227 --> 00:09:01.060 align:middle line:84%
How many of these things
do you need to buy?

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:02.020 align:middle line:90%
How much do they cost?

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:02.978 align:middle line:90%
All that kind of stuff.

00:09:02.978 --> 00:09:05.114 align:middle line:90%
How reusable are they?

00:09:05.114 --> 00:09:07.140 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.127 align:middle line:90%


00:09:10.127 --> 00:09:11.710 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: OK,
this is really good.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:13.002 align:middle line:90%
Why don't we take a break here?

00:09:13.002 --> 00:09:14.960 align:middle line:84%
Go ahead and you can sit
back down to the seats

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:15.840 align:middle line:90%
that you were at.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.370 align:middle line:84%
I think everyone's written
kind of broadly enough

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:23.460 align:middle line:84%
that we'll be able to
see from a distance.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:25.210 align:middle line:90%
But yeah, really good.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:25.710 align:middle line:90%
Really good.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.543 align:middle line:84%
Now I need to find a
little spot for me to--

00:09:27.543 --> 00:09:30.390 align:middle line:90%


00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:32.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Everyone's
last minute notes.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.082 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, yeah.

00:09:33.082 --> 00:09:38.320 align:middle line:84%
Now's your chance to put the
final few things up there.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:38.820 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.210 align:middle line:84%
So what was the minimum
number of stakeholders people

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:43.950 align:middle line:90%
came up with?

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.210 align:middle line:84%
These guys are the winners
with the longest list.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.050 align:middle line:90%
Let's just read it out loud.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:48.550 align:middle line:90%
Oh, no.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:49.050 align:middle line:90%
The state.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.370 align:middle line:84%
Admin, parents, teachers,
students, corporations,

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.690 align:middle line:84%
manufacturers, edtech investors,
developers, policymakers.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:55.800 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.520 align:middle line:90%
So just as a starting point--

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.062 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are some other
ones that showed up around here.

00:10:00.062 --> 00:10:04.700 align:middle line:84%
Studios, broadcasters, the FCC
that different-- psychologists.

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:05.200 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.680 align:middle line:84%
There are a lot of
people who have something

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.670 align:middle line:90%
at stake in these decisions.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:12.790 align:middle line:84%
They're complex, multi-human
kinds of decisions.

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.130 align:middle line:90%
So that's a good start.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.230 align:middle line:90%
Let me just read.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:18.660 align:middle line:84%
So given how complex these
things are, why are folks

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:19.840 align:middle line:90%
so enthusiastic about it?

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:22.510 align:middle line:90%
Let's ask Thomas Edison.

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.490 align:middle line:84%
What does Thomas Edison
think about this?

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:27.570 align:middle line:84%
I believe that the motion
picture is destined

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:31.590 align:middle line:84%
to revolutionize our educational
system, and that in a few years,

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:35.980 align:middle line:84%
it will supplant largely, if not
entirely, the use of textbooks.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:37.570 align:middle line:84%
I should say that
on the average,

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:40.080 align:middle line:84%
we get about 2% efficiency
out of school books

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.580 align:middle line:90%
as they are written today.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:42.760 align:middle line:90%
You're laughing.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.180 align:middle line:90%
Why are you laughing?

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.290 align:middle line:84%
Thomas Edison is just
here making shit up.

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:48.490 align:middle line:90%
Like, he doesn't--

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:49.220 align:middle line:90%
2%?

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:50.030 align:middle line:90%
That doesn't mean a thing.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:51.155 align:middle line:90%
He was good at light bulbs.

00:10:51.155 --> 00:10:52.930 align:middle line:90%
He wasn't maybe as good at this.

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:54.970 align:middle line:84%
The education of
the future as I see

00:10:54.970 --> 00:10:57.370 align:middle line:84%
it will be conducted through
the medium of the motion

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:00.160 align:middle line:84%
picture, where it should
be possible to obtain

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:02.540 align:middle line:90%
100% efficiency.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.020 align:middle line:90%
Thomas Edison, 1922.

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:06.520 align:middle line:84%
You'll read a couple
of other quotes

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.000 align:middle line:90%
that I have from Thomas Edison.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.298 align:middle line:84%
One of them is in here,
in "Failure to Disrupt"

00:11:10.298 --> 00:11:11.090 align:middle line:90%
when you read that.

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:14.590 align:middle line:84%
But in 1913, he went in front of
Congress and said, in 10 years,

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:18.740 align:middle line:84%
the textbook will be entirely
replaced by filmstrips.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:21.920 align:middle line:84%
And then he went back
10 years later in 1923

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:24.640 align:middle line:84%
and he said, in 20
years, textbooks

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:27.490 align:middle line:84%
will be entirely
replaced by filmstrips.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:31.480 align:middle line:84%
I think you can find many
similar kinds of arguments

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:32.980 align:middle line:90%
that are made today.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:36.700 align:middle line:84%
I think you will not have a hard
time finding people who tell you

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:39.130 align:middle line:84%
that the old fashioned
techniques are only 2%

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:42.130 align:middle line:84%
efficient, but the new ones
will be 100% efficient.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:43.190 align:middle line:90%
So that's the promise.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:45.980 align:middle line:84%
The promise is that we could do
substantially better than we're

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:49.400 align:middle line:84%
doing right now if we
think about implementing

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.480 align:middle line:84%
all of these different
kinds of technologies.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.850 align:middle line:84%
So an intuition
that you can develop

00:11:55.850 --> 00:12:00.560 align:middle line:84%
is that this is a
century old enterprise.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.860 align:middle line:84%
For the last 100,
110 years, people

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:06.110 align:middle line:84%
have thought, how can we
take analog technologies

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:07.640 align:middle line:84%
and eventually
digital technologies

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:10.520 align:middle line:84%
bring them into schools
to improve learning?

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:13.260 align:middle line:84%
There are millions of people
who work in education.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:16.130 align:middle line:84%
Many of them are
very, very smart.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:19.640 align:middle line:84%
From the earliest-- you read
a little bit about personal

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:22.657 align:middle line:84%
computers, the kind of from the
'70s and '80s, the Apple II plus

00:12:22.657 --> 00:12:24.990 align:middle line:84%
kind of the generation of
computers that I grew up with.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:28.190 align:middle line:84%
But that's actually, like,
20 years into the game.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:32.060 align:middle line:84%
From the moment people built
computers the size of this room,

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:34.395 align:middle line:84%
computer scientists,
educators, learning scientists

00:12:34.395 --> 00:12:36.020 align:middle line:84%
partnered together
to try to figure out

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:38.430 align:middle line:84%
if there's some way to use
computers to teach people.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:41.190 align:middle line:84%
So just compute, not even
all these other radio,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:42.210 align:middle line:90%
film, other things.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:45.300 align:middle line:84%
Computing alone is like
a 70 year enterprise

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:46.810 align:middle line:90%
of trying to improve schools.

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:49.500 align:middle line:84%
There are lots of things
that we've tried and learned

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.350 align:middle line:90%
in 70 years.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.250 align:middle line:84%
So one intuition that I hope you
will take away from this class

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:56.310 align:middle line:84%
is that when you're thinking
about an education technology

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:58.860 align:middle line:84%
problem of any form or
type, about a design

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:01.330 align:middle line:84%
problem, an implementation
problem, a policy problem,

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:03.000 align:middle line:84%
you should think
to yourself, we've

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.330 align:middle line:84%
probably have done
something like this before.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:09.790 align:middle line:84%
There is nothing that is so
new, so utterly different,

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.910 align:middle line:84%
that there aren't
cognate examples that

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:15.300 align:middle line:84%
could help us think through
whatever these challenges are.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:19.150 align:middle line:84%
Let's think about a couple
of these stakeholders--

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:22.320 align:middle line:84%
which stakeholders seem
to appear pretty much

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.810 align:middle line:90%
in all of them?

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:28.098 align:middle line:84%
Who seem to be
universal stakeholders?

00:13:28.098 --> 00:13:28.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Teachers.

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:29.848 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Teachers.

00:13:29.848 --> 00:13:32.070 align:middle line:84%
Let's start with them,
because they're the best.

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:33.730 align:middle line:90%
I love teachers.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:36.990 align:middle line:84%
I mean, definitely one of the
reasons why I do what I do

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.870 align:middle line:84%
is I just think
teachers are great.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.940 align:middle line:90%


00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.971 align:middle line:84%
What kinds of things
get teachers excited?

00:13:46.971 --> 00:13:49.900 align:middle line:84%
If you want to get a teacher
using education technology,

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:52.290 align:middle line:84%
what are the felt
needs that they

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.550 align:middle line:84%
have that are going to be
really important to them?

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:56.280 align:middle line:84%
This is one that I think
will be pretty consistent

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:57.630 align:middle line:84%
across a bunch of
these, but we can see

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:58.605 align:middle line:90%
if there are differences too.

00:13:58.605 --> 00:14:00.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Reducing
their workload.

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:02.530 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: OK,
good I love it.

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:05.370 align:middle line:90%
Reducing workload.

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:08.072 align:middle line:84%
Why is that so
important to teachers?

00:14:08.072 --> 00:14:09.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just because
they have so much.

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:11.363 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: They
have so much to do.

00:14:11.363 --> 00:14:13.770 align:middle line:84%
These are not people who
spend a bunch of time sitting

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:16.470 align:middle line:84%
around waiting for the phone
to ring or something like that.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:18.390 align:middle line:84%
Teacher workload in
the United States

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:21.090 align:middle line:84%
is actually, in certain
ways, much higher

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:23.110 align:middle line:84%
than in other comparable
OECD countries.

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:25.235 align:middle line:84%
So OECD is the Organization
of Economic Cooperative

00:14:25.235 --> 00:14:26.610 align:middle line:84%
Development,
something like that.

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:28.785 align:middle line:84%
It's like rich countries,
30 or 40 rich countries.

00:14:28.785 --> 00:14:30.660 align:middle line:84%
American teachers,
compared to our colleagues

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:33.960 align:middle line:84%
in other countries, spend
more time in front of students

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:36.570 align:middle line:84%
actually directly teaching
than in many other places.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:39.118 align:middle line:84%
So many other countries
in the world, a full time

00:14:39.118 --> 00:14:41.410 align:middle line:84%
licensed teacher would have
more collaborative planning

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:43.820 align:middle line:84%
time, more grading time, other
kinds of things like that.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:45.653 align:middle line:84%
So US teachers, compared
to their colleagues

00:14:45.653 --> 00:14:49.120 align:middle line:84%
around the world, are behind
the ball on having time anyway.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:51.110 align:middle line:84%
Reducing workload is
something they care about.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:52.788 align:middle line:90%
What else do they care about?

00:14:52.788 --> 00:14:54.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It has to spark
the student's interest.

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:55.180 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:56.790 align:middle line:84%
So they don't want
kids to be bored.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:15:00.313 align:middle line:90%


00:15:00.313 --> 00:15:02.230 align:middle line:84%
One of their core interests
is, how am I going

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:03.380 align:middle line:90%
to keep these folks motivated?

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:04.280 align:middle line:84%
How am I going to
keep them learning?

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:05.680 align:middle line:90%
What else do they care about?

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They care about it
being actually good quality.

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:09.138 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK, good.

00:15:09.138 --> 00:15:13.250 align:middle line:84%
So it's really important
that learning goes up,

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:18.220 align:middle line:84%
that quality is
high, that teachers

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:20.540 align:middle line:84%
take their professionalism
very, very seriously

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:24.100 align:middle line:84%
and they're held accountable
in various kinds of ways.

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:26.260 align:middle line:84%
And this could be different
across different kinds

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:27.160 align:middle line:90%
of teachers.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:29.575 align:middle line:84%
So in the United
States, we primarily--

00:15:29.575 --> 00:15:32.390 align:middle line:84%
we test-- in certain
kinds of ways,

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.520 align:middle line:84%
we test way more than
every other country does.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:36.550 align:middle line:84%
There's no country
that tests all

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:38.860 align:middle line:84%
of their students every
year, third to eighth grade,

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:40.098 align:middle line:90%
in both reading and math.

00:15:40.098 --> 00:15:42.140 align:middle line:84%
Most other countries are
like, if you tested them

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:43.723 align:middle line:84%
in the third grade,
you would probably

00:15:43.723 --> 00:15:44.795 align:middle line:90%
wait a couple years to--

00:15:44.795 --> 00:15:45.840 align:middle line:90%
I mean, you give them--

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.360 align:middle line:84%
but you give them assessments
or exams or whatever,

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:50.750 align:middle line:84%
but you don't give them
big national tests.

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:55.040 align:middle line:84%
And so if you are a teacher
of reading or of math,

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:57.830 align:middle line:84%
your test scores are
scrutinized in a way

00:15:57.830 --> 00:16:01.500 align:middle line:84%
that a science teacher, art
teacher, a health teacher,

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:04.230 align:middle line:84%
other kinds of teachers are
not similarly scrutinized.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:07.350 align:middle line:84%
So within, there can be
different kinds of incentives.

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:09.710 align:middle line:84%
One of the things I always
enjoyed about being a history

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:12.500 align:middle line:84%
teacher is that there
were never really tests

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:15.525 align:middle line:84%
that I were preparing my
students for in the same way--

00:16:15.525 --> 00:16:18.150 align:middle line:84%
I taught in private school, but
even my math teacher colleagues

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:20.070 align:middle line:84%
felt like they were preparing
students for the SAT,

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:21.360 align:middle line:90%
for the APs, things like that.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:23.210 align:middle line:84%
That was never
really my concern.

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:26.580 align:middle line:84%
As a result, I had to spend
less time thinking to myself,

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:28.890 align:middle line:84%
this new thing I'm bringing
into this classroom,

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:32.450 align:middle line:84%
is this going to prepare
people for some kind of nearly

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:33.870 align:middle line:90%
universal testing experiences?

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.150 align:middle line:84%
Like my math teacher
colleagues always

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:36.400 align:middle line:90%
had to be thinking about that.

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:39.652 align:middle line:84%
I could be like, well, Tibetan
Buddhism isn't on the test,

00:16:39.652 --> 00:16:40.360 align:middle line:90%
but it's awesome.

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:42.160 align:middle line:90%
Let's study it.

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:43.480 align:middle line:90%
So that's different.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:43.980 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:44.897 align:middle line:90%
Other kinds of things.

00:16:44.897 --> 00:16:46.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Tai and then Sabrina.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Accessibility
and inclusion.

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:50.520 align:middle line:84%
So to make sure that
no one's left out

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:53.080 align:middle line:84%
from your diverse group of
learners from any technology.

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:53.872 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:16:53.872 --> 00:16:55.920 align:middle line:90%


00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:00.960 align:middle line:84%
And that would not be
a universal concern

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.370 align:middle line:90%
across these decades.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:05.490 align:middle line:90%


00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:08.790 align:middle line:84%
It would have been considered
much more common to say,

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:12.300 align:middle line:84%
certain people have
totally unsolvable learning

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.480 align:middle line:84%
disabilities, and societies
should tend to their welfare

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:18.160 align:middle line:84%
in some other kind of way in
the '30s and '40s and '50s.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:20.368 align:middle line:84%
And then somewhere in the
middle of the 20th century,

00:17:20.368 --> 00:17:22.950 align:middle line:84%
we passed the Americans
with Disabilities Act,

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.890 align:middle line:84%
which totally transformed
our thoughts and in fact,

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:30.060 align:middle line:84%
created a federal right for
people to have a way of--

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:32.670 align:middle line:84%
a fair and appropriate
public education.

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:37.590 align:middle line:84%
And I would also just say,
as society has gotten more

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:40.680 align:middle line:84%
interested in values
of inclusion generally,

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:43.710 align:middle line:84%
what people would have worried
about with inclusion with

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:46.560 align:middle line:84%
personal computers in the 1980s
is very different from what they

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.240 align:middle line:84%
would be worried about with
the computers that are getting

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:49.782 align:middle line:84%
in front of their
students right now.

00:17:49.782 --> 00:17:50.610 align:middle line:90%
That's a great one.

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:54.012 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm going to say being
able to track student progress.

00:17:54.012 --> 00:17:55.220 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Track progress.

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:58.050 align:middle line:90%


00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.090 align:middle line:90%
Assessment.

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:01.590 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:02.250 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:03.167 align:middle line:90%
Actually, interesting.

00:18:03.167 --> 00:18:07.350 align:middle line:84%
Over this century, some of
the earliest technologies

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:09.340 align:middle line:84%
had nothing to do with
that, radio and film.

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:10.882 align:middle line:84%
But one of the very
earliest ones you

00:18:10.882 --> 00:18:14.190 align:middle line:84%
read about, physical
teaching machines--

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:17.280 align:middle line:84%
and hopefully, I mean,
you saw some pictures.

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:19.400 align:middle line:84%
I was just actually
looking today.

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:21.900 align:middle line:84%
Sidney Pressey is one of the
people who Audrey Waters writes

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.360 align:middle line:84%
about, and there's
somebody on YouTube

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:28.243 align:middle line:84%
who had posted this fabulous two
minute clip of him demonstrating

00:18:28.243 --> 00:18:29.410 align:middle line:90%
one of his teacher machines.

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:31.202 align:middle line:84%
And unfortunately, that
account was deleted

00:18:31.202 --> 00:18:33.300 align:middle line:90%
before this year's class.

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:35.050 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll send you a
version of it later.

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:36.508 align:middle line:84%
The only version
of the video still

00:18:36.508 --> 00:18:41.210 align:middle line:84%
exists is one in which Anant
Agarwal's voice is superimposed

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:41.900 align:middle line:90%
of it.

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:45.110 align:middle line:84%
So somebody took an audio
clip of Anant Agarwal talking

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:48.383 align:middle line:84%
about the capacities of
edx and played the audio

00:18:48.383 --> 00:18:50.300 align:middle line:84%
over Sidney Pressey
demonstrating the teaching

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:51.380 align:middle line:90%
machine.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.990 align:middle line:84%
And it's a little too
close for comfort,

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:56.430 align:middle line:84%
even though it's
a century apart.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.510 align:middle line:84%
But teaching machines, they
were kind of like typewriters.

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:01.350 align:middle line:84%
They had these four
keys that were on them,

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:04.400 align:middle line:84%
and so they would display
one question at a time.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:06.680 align:middle line:84%
It was like
pre-Scantron technology,

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:10.010 align:middle line:84%
and then there would be one
of four possible answers.

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:11.940 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the
earliest versions.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:14.505 align:middle line:84%
It had a little bank of
mints in the back of them,

00:19:14.505 --> 00:19:16.130 align:middle line:84%
and so when you press
the right button,

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.422 align:middle line:84%
a little candy or something
would roll out and give you

00:19:18.422 --> 00:19:22.505 align:middle line:84%
a little behaviorist positive
reinforcement kinds of things.

00:19:22.505 --> 00:19:25.560 align:middle line:84%
But one of the main virtues
of it was like, oh, man,

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:28.700 align:middle line:84%
if we could get enough
people these things,

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:31.925 align:middle line:84%
then we could assess their
progress almost constantly.

00:19:31.925 --> 00:19:33.300 align:middle line:84%
We'd know exactly
where they are.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:36.390 align:middle line:90%
So this idea of tracking--

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:38.180 align:middle line:84%
tracking student
progress is something

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:40.782 align:middle line:84%
that's like particularly
urgently concerned

00:19:40.782 --> 00:19:43.490 align:middle line:84%
in our data driven world but was
still something that people were

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:46.010 align:middle line:90%
interested in a century ago.

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:47.290 align:middle line:90%
What are some of the--

00:19:47.290 --> 00:19:48.540 align:middle line:90%
I'll just put them under here.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:52.140 align:middle line:84%
What are some of the would-be
disincentives for teachers?

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:53.900 align:middle line:84%
What are some things
that if you were

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:55.958 align:middle line:84%
trying to get teachers to
adopt new technologies,

00:19:55.958 --> 00:19:58.500 align:middle line:84%
would be the sorts of things
that would be barriers for them?

00:19:58.500 --> 00:19:59.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Complexity.

00:19:59.390 --> 00:20:02.150 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Why complexity?

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:06.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's some teachers
who are more able to adapt

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.330 align:middle line:84%
to new technologies, and
some teachers who just cannot

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.868 align:middle line:84%
understand how certain
technologies might work.

00:20:10.868 --> 00:20:11.660 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:16.130 align:middle line:84%
So I think that
aptitude is right.

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:19.663 align:middle line:84%
If you go into teaching,
there are probably two things

00:20:19.663 --> 00:20:20.580 align:middle line:90%
that really drive you.

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:22.653 align:middle line:84%
One is you love the
content that you teach.

00:20:22.653 --> 00:20:24.320 align:middle line:84%
That's more true for
secondary educators

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.630 align:middle line:84%
than for early elementary
educators, but probably true

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:27.180 align:middle line:90%
for them.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:28.958 align:middle line:90%
And you probably love people.

00:20:28.958 --> 00:20:30.500 align:middle line:84%
Very difficult to
be a teacher if you

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:33.270 align:middle line:84%
don't find people, especially
young people, pretty engaging.

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:35.323 align:middle line:84%
The kinds of folks who
find people engaging,

00:20:35.323 --> 00:20:36.240 align:middle line:90%
they might just find--

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.640 align:middle line:84%
it's not 100% overlap, but
there's just technology is less

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:39.130 align:middle line:90%
engaging.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:40.560 align:middle line:84%
They didn't want to spend
their life sitting in front

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:41.810 align:middle line:90%
of a computer typing in stuff.

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:45.150 align:middle line:84%
They wanted to be
interacting with people.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:46.980 align:middle line:84%
But the other thing
I would say is

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:50.400 align:middle line:84%
that there are many educators
who spent a lot of time

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:53.047 align:middle line:84%
polishing their craft and are
good at teaching the way they're

00:20:53.047 --> 00:20:55.630 align:middle line:84%
teaching right now and have been
teaching for the last decade.

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:58.240 align:middle line:84%
And so when they see a new
piece of technology come in,

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:00.420 align:middle line:84%
they're thinking to themselves,
is this thing really

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:02.670 align:middle line:84%
going to be better than the
thing that I've been doing

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:06.120 align:middle line:90%
and got good at for a while?

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:08.220 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to have to
lose something when

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:09.700 align:middle line:90%
I integrate this new thing?

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:12.220 align:middle line:84%
Like any technology
adoption involves loss.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:13.360 align:middle line:90%
Any change involves loss.

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:15.390 align:middle line:90%
You got to give something up.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:19.120 align:middle line:84%
That thing I'm giving up,
it may be pretty good.

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:22.272 align:middle line:84%
I think a lot of people
think that older teachers are

00:21:22.272 --> 00:21:24.480 align:middle line:84%
less likely to adopt
technologies because they're not

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:25.300 align:middle line:90%
technology fluent.

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:27.455 align:middle line:84%
But having worked with
many, many teachers,

00:21:27.455 --> 00:21:28.830 align:middle line:84%
I'm really convinced
that there's

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:31.457 align:middle line:84%
no relationship between age
and technology adoption.

00:21:31.457 --> 00:21:33.540 align:middle line:84%
There are young teachers
that are super interested

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:34.810 align:middle line:84%
and there are young
teachers who hate it,

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:37.630 align:middle line:84%
their old teachers who are like,
I'm pretty good at what I do.

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:39.720 align:middle line:84%
And there are old teachers who
are old teachers because they've

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.178 align:middle line:84%
spent the last 20
years going, it's

00:21:41.178 --> 00:21:42.430 align:middle line:90%
really fun to try new things.

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:44.050 align:middle line:84%
It's really fun to
try to get better.

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:46.150 align:middle line:84%
What was one of your
disincentives, Sabrina?

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:47.770 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh,
student distraction.

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:50.160 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Great.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:52.620 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, some
of these technologies

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:54.110 align:middle line:84%
are more akin to
that than others.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:56.610 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you're playing a film
strip or something like that,

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.520 align:middle line:84%
you probably have kids'
attention or things like that.

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:02.380 align:middle line:84%
But certainly as we
get into computers,

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:06.630 align:middle line:84%
anything in which the attention
is-- where each student is

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:08.670 align:middle line:84%
responsible for
their own attention

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:11.640 align:middle line:84%
is something that
can raise concerns

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:13.320 align:middle line:84%
for teachers, because
kids like to be

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:15.450 align:middle line:90%
attend to all kinds of things.

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:17.638 align:middle line:84%
What other things
would be disincentives?

00:22:17.638 --> 00:22:19.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like,
teacher displacement.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:20.972 align:middle line:84%
Like they feel like
they're being replaced.

00:22:20.972 --> 00:22:22.770 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yep.

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:29.410 align:middle line:84%
So teachers are a profession
that protects their job security

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:30.910 align:middle line:84%
with a license and
things like that,

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:32.327 align:middle line:84%
like lots of other
professions do.

00:22:32.327 --> 00:22:36.950 align:middle line:84%
Like doctors and pharmacists and
lawyers and all kinds of people.

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:38.450 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, most
professionals are not

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.890 align:middle line:84%
super enthusiastic
about technology

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:41.930 align:middle line:84%
that has the capacity to replace
the functions that they're

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:42.430 align:middle line:90%
doing.

00:22:42.430 --> 00:22:44.880 align:middle line:90%


00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:45.380 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:48.320 align:middle line:84%
Let's think about
some of these other--

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:49.980 align:middle line:90%
so this is a good one.

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:51.800 align:middle line:84%
So again, we can
think about any time

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:53.880 align:middle line:84%
a new technology is
being introduced,

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.480 align:middle line:90%
is it going to reduce workload?

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:56.730 align:middle line:90%
Is it going to spark interest?

00:22:56.730 --> 00:22:58.453 align:middle line:84%
Will it spark interest
in the first three

00:22:58.453 --> 00:23:00.120 align:middle line:84%
weeks of this new
technology being here?

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:02.420 align:middle line:84%
Will it spark interest two
years after the technology

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:03.330 align:middle line:90%
has been introduced?

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:04.860 align:middle line:84%
Is it going to be
of high quality?

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.040 align:middle line:84%
Do I have control
over the quality?

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:09.660 align:middle line:84%
Accessible, inclusion, is it
going to improve learning?

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:11.250 align:middle line:84%
Will it be able to
track assessment?

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:12.510 align:middle line:90%
Is it too complex?

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.398 align:middle line:90%
Will it distract my students?

00:23:14.398 --> 00:23:16.190 align:middle line:84%
Is it going to displace
essential functions

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:18.200 align:middle line:90%
that I perform?

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.750 align:middle line:84%
That is a good set of
questions that you could ask,

00:23:20.750 --> 00:23:23.840 align:middle line:84%
Cuban does ask in some ways in
radio and film and television

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:25.640 align:middle line:84%
and teaching machines
and computers.

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:29.038 align:middle line:84%
And you could ask them of AI
and Khan Academy and iCivics

00:23:29.038 --> 00:23:31.580 align:middle line:84%
and anything else that comes
along, like, these are all still

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:33.350 align:middle line:90%
good questions to ask.

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:36.950 align:middle line:84%
Let's try to hit now a bunch
of these other stakeholders.

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:39.270 align:middle line:84%
Who are some of the
stakeholders that surprised you?

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:40.550 align:middle line:84%
Who are the ones that
you were like, whoa, I

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:43.175 align:middle line:84%
wouldn't have thought of this in
terms of education technology?

00:23:43.175 --> 00:23:46.400 align:middle line:90%


00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:50.030 align:middle line:90%
Weird ones or unique ones?

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:51.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I mean, I thought--

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.520 align:middle line:84%
my group was the
teacher machines,

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.610 align:middle line:84%
and it was the
psychologists that

00:23:55.610 --> 00:24:01.730 align:middle line:84%
come up with the ideas that
go into how these technologies

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:04.490 align:middle line:90%
are-- or what they're based on.

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:06.320 align:middle line:84%
I guess I just
never really thought

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:10.130 align:middle line:84%
like, oh, it is the people
who thought of constructivism

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:12.350 align:middle line:84%
and situated learning
that has then

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:15.200 align:middle line:84%
sparked this interest
in everything else.

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:18.020 align:middle line:84%
And so it's like people
have ideas but all of that

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:20.370 align:middle line:84%
initially stems from these
two thoughts of learning.

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:20.660 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:21.160 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:24:21.160 --> 00:24:23.855 align:middle line:84%
So people have ideas about
what good teaching and learning

00:24:23.855 --> 00:24:24.480 align:middle line:90%
should be like.

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.245 align:middle line:84%
Those ideas are
in some ways they

00:24:26.245 --> 00:24:28.620 align:middle line:84%
seem pretty universal because
we can find them going back

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:29.287 align:middle line:90%
through history.

00:24:29.287 --> 00:24:32.200 align:middle line:84%
But they're always being
reinterpreted, updated,

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:33.510 align:middle line:90%
things like that.

00:24:33.510 --> 00:24:35.430 align:middle line:84%
Sidney Pressey is
in this moment where

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:39.827 align:middle line:84%
behaviorism is an incredibly
prominent set of ideas.

00:24:39.827 --> 00:24:41.910 align:middle line:84%
Behaviorism, you're probably
all familiar with of.

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:44.850 align:middle line:84%
You punish people when they do
things wrong and you reward them

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:46.710 align:middle line:84%
when you do things
right and you condition

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:48.330 align:middle line:90%
their behavior that way.

00:24:48.330 --> 00:24:50.520 align:middle line:84%
And so he creates
a physical machine

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:53.490 align:middle line:90%
that embodies those ideas.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:57.240 align:middle line:84%
and then Seymour Papert
will come along and be

00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:59.490 align:middle line:84%
like, oh, man, why can't
we learn math the way

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:02.880 align:middle line:84%
that kids who go to visit
France learn French?

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:04.810 align:middle line:84%
Why can't we make a
thing which is mathland?

00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:06.512 align:middle line:84%
I bet Logo could
be like mathland.

00:25:06.512 --> 00:25:08.970 align:middle line:84%
He has a set of ideas about
what good teaching and learning

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:13.560 align:middle line:84%
looks like, and he incorporates
those ideas and people become

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:16.230 align:middle line:90%
partisans of these ideas.

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:19.470 align:middle line:84%
Probably the most
prominent partisan

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:21.390 align:middle line:84%
of education technology
in the United States

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:23.640 align:middle line:84%
today is Sal Khan,
who is someone

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:27.390 align:middle line:84%
who had a very particular
idea, a clear vision of what

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:29.338 align:middle line:84%
personalized learning
would look like.

00:25:29.338 --> 00:25:31.630 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you know
about his personal experience,

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:33.838 align:middle line:84%
but he was a hedge fund
person or something like that

00:25:33.838 --> 00:25:37.150 align:middle line:84%
and his niece was having
trouble with math,

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:39.510 align:middle line:84%
and he started tutoring
his niece in math.

00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:40.427 align:middle line:90%
He did it over videos.

00:25:40.427 --> 00:25:43.093 align:middle line:84%
And so he put the videos online
and people started watching them

00:25:43.093 --> 00:25:44.910 align:middle line:84%
and go, wow, what if
every person could

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:47.580 align:middle line:84%
have this little like,
personalized tutor?

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:49.530 align:middle line:84%
What if each person
could study math

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.540 align:middle line:90%
at their own individual pace?

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:54.555 align:middle line:84%
And then he built this whole
enterprise and continues to--

00:25:54.555 --> 00:25:56.910 align:middle line:84%
he's incredibly
enthusiastic about AI

00:25:56.910 --> 00:25:58.950 align:middle line:84%
right now because,
oh, man, instead

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:02.580 align:middle line:84%
of just having me record a
tutor for each person, what

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:06.300 align:middle line:84%
if the GPT can generate
personalized text related

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:09.000 align:middle line:84%
to each individual person
or something like that?

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:12.630 align:middle line:84%
And those ideas, it's
not like they're just

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:13.930 align:middle line:90%
dropped in the world.

00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:15.073 align:middle line:90%
They're advocated for.

00:26:15.073 --> 00:26:17.490 align:middle line:84%
Did you all see the New York
Times-- was it New York Times

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:18.282 align:middle line:90%
or Washington Post?

00:26:18.282 --> 00:26:21.890 align:middle line:84%
Somebody wrote an op ed
about Sal Khan and AI.

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:24.390 align:middle line:84%
It was either in the Washington
Post and the New York Times,

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:26.330 align:middle line:84%
but it was basically
something like, this

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:28.520 align:middle line:84%
is the first AI that
your kid will love

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:31.490 align:middle line:84%
or something like that, and it's
basically just celebratory piece

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:33.530 align:middle line:90%
of Khanmigo.

00:26:33.530 --> 00:26:34.260 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:35.930 align:middle line:90%
So that's an unusual group.

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:41.777 align:middle line:84%
And those groups, I mean, today,
Sal Khan goes on The Today Show

00:26:41.777 --> 00:26:43.110 align:middle line:90%
and tells people what he thinks.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:45.777 align:middle line:84%
He goes to investor conferences
and tells people what he thinks.

00:26:45.777 --> 00:26:46.582 align:middle line:90%
He goes to schools.

00:26:46.582 --> 00:26:48.290 align:middle line:84%
Harvard and MIT bring
him in every couple

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:49.540 align:middle line:90%
of years and things like that.

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:53.005 align:middle line:84%
So these folks advocate
for their ideas.

00:26:53.005 --> 00:26:54.630 align:middle line:84%
The ideas don't just
fall from the sky.

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:58.730 align:middle line:84%
There's a stakeholder who
is advancing those ideas.

00:26:58.730 --> 00:27:01.442 align:middle line:84%
Who are some other stakeholders
that seem important?

00:27:01.442 --> 00:27:03.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I thought it was
weird that you could think

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:06.950 align:middle line:84%
about manufacturers and
I mean, the people who

00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:10.170 align:middle line:84%
produce transistors, which
is like TSMC and ASML,

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:13.040 align:middle line:84%
because if a bunch of schools
across America all of a sudden

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:15.410 align:middle line:84%
need thousands and
thousands of iPads

00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:18.620 align:middle line:84%
and you have this big
backlog request on M2 chips

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:20.130 align:middle line:84%
or whatever is in
them right now,

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:22.250 align:middle line:84%
and then someone like
TSMC, the only people

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:24.650 align:middle line:84%
who really produce transistors,
has this huge float

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:25.860 align:middle line:90%
and make a lot of money.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:26.902 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, yeah.

00:27:26.902 --> 00:27:30.470 align:middle line:84%
That people become-- that those
folks then organize themselves

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:34.580 align:middle line:84%
into trade groups
and they go to--

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:36.960 align:middle line:84%
how do they advance
their interests?

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:41.090 align:middle line:84%
They do things like give
some of their materials away

00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:42.390 align:middle line:90%
for schools, demonstration.

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:45.110 align:middle line:84%
They create training
programs for their materials

00:27:45.110 --> 00:27:46.340 align:middle line:90%
and share them.

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:48.290 align:middle line:84%
They have sales and
marketing people

00:27:48.290 --> 00:27:51.020 align:middle line:84%
that go around and get
people advanced about that.

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:55.220 align:middle line:84%
They sponsor conferences,
maybe that they're not even

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:56.550 align:middle line:90%
directly advertising for.

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:58.910 align:middle line:84%
They're just their
presence to try

00:27:58.910 --> 00:28:04.460 align:middle line:84%
to encourage teachers and other
sorts of groups to be behind it.

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:07.490 align:middle line:84%
We spend an enormous amount of
money on education every year

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:10.640 align:middle line:84%
in the United States, and the
biggest chunk of that money

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:12.990 align:middle line:84%
goes to pay the salaries
of the people who do stuff.

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:14.448 align:middle line:84%
And another big
chunk of that money

00:28:14.448 --> 00:28:16.010 align:middle line:84%
goes to heating and
air conditioning,

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:18.360 align:middle line:84%
for which there are people
who are selling those things.

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:22.160 align:middle line:84%
But basically the third
biggest bucket after those two

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:24.930 align:middle line:84%
is the stuff we buy in
schools to help people learn.

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:29.730 align:middle line:84%
And so yeah, there are
advocates who are--

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:33.420 align:middle line:84%
the folks who are not
neutral in these things.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:35.407 align:middle line:84%
Apple computer has been
particularly prominent

00:28:35.407 --> 00:28:36.990 align:middle line:84%
over the last bunch
of years in trying

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:39.570 align:middle line:84%
to craft a vision of
itself as a company that

00:28:39.570 --> 00:28:43.360 align:middle line:84%
has a particular role inside
colleges, universities, schools,

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:45.913 align:middle line:90%
those kinds of things.

00:28:45.913 --> 00:28:47.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think something
that surprised me,

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:49.288 align:middle line:84%
but maybe it's not
that surprising,

00:28:49.288 --> 00:28:52.980 align:middle line:84%
was that governments
as a whole and leaders

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:56.760 align:middle line:84%
in between governments,
because in the class we took

00:28:56.760 --> 00:28:58.810 align:middle line:84%
last semester
intro to education,

00:28:58.810 --> 00:29:00.450 align:middle line:84%
we talked a lot
about Americanism

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:02.217 align:middle line:84%
and how it's so
linked with education.

00:29:02.217 --> 00:29:04.050 align:middle line:84%
And I know it's such a
big thing to be like,

00:29:04.050 --> 00:29:07.290 align:middle line:84%
oh, this country has the
biggest-- or the highest SAT

00:29:07.290 --> 00:29:08.963 align:middle line:84%
score on average
or this many people

00:29:08.963 --> 00:29:10.630 align:middle line:84%
use Khan Academy or
something like that.

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:13.590 align:middle line:84%
So sometimes it really
is just like governments

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:15.510 align:middle line:84%
competing against
each other and trying

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:17.117 align:middle line:84%
to see who's the
best of the best.

00:29:17.117 --> 00:29:17.950 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Great.

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:18.870 align:middle line:84%
And in the United
States, when we

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:20.623 align:middle line:84%
talk about government
actors, what are all

00:29:20.623 --> 00:29:22.290 align:middle line:84%
the different possible
government actors

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:25.500 align:middle line:90%
we might be thinking about?

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:28.740 align:middle line:84%
So there's a US
Department of Education.

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:29.690 align:middle line:90%
Who else is there?

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:32.065 align:middle line:90%


00:29:32.065 --> 00:29:33.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So local
boards of education.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:35.815 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So there's
local boards of education.

00:29:35.815 --> 00:29:38.670 align:middle line:84%
So a very unusual thing about
the United States Department

00:29:38.670 --> 00:29:40.920 align:middle line:84%
of Education is it is-- or
the United States education

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.270 align:middle line:84%
system is it's
extremely decentralized.

00:29:43.270 --> 00:29:46.020 align:middle line:84%
There are 130,000 schools
and there are 13,000 school

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:46.660 align:middle line:90%
districts.

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:48.870 align:middle line:84%
The modal elected official
in the United States

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:51.315 align:middle line:90%
is a school board member.

00:29:51.315 --> 00:29:53.440 align:middle line:84%
If you're an elected official
in the United States,

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:56.580 align:middle line:84%
the thing you're most likely
to be is a school board member.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:02.685 align:middle line:84%
And school board members are
interesting stakeholders.

00:30:02.685 --> 00:30:04.560 align:middle line:84%
And they range from
community to communities.

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:07.410 align:middle line:84%
In lots of communities,
they're just a community member

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:09.690 align:middle line:84%
who's particularly
interested in education,

00:30:09.690 --> 00:30:13.950 align:middle line:84%
but they're also people
who are looking to--

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:15.420 align:middle line:84%
it's an entry point
for people who

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:16.960 align:middle line:84%
are interested in
political careers.

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:18.752 align:middle line:84%
Lots of people who are
school board members

00:30:18.752 --> 00:30:21.320 align:middle line:84%
go to be on state reps and
other kinds of things like that.

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:25.270 align:middle line:84%
This is a particularly
fecund moment

00:30:25.270 --> 00:30:31.060 align:middle line:84%
for people who are politically
ambitious to pursue

00:30:31.060 --> 00:30:33.640 align:middle line:84%
those ambitions through
the school system,

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:38.560 align:middle line:84%
especially in the American
right, where reform of schools

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:41.290 align:middle line:84%
has become a way to
get kind of attention

00:30:41.290 --> 00:30:44.950 align:middle line:84%
within the political party
and things like that.

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:48.820 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, there's all kinds of
towns across the country where--

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:51.555 align:middle line:84%
and I think you get some of
this is some school board is,

00:30:51.555 --> 00:30:53.680 align:middle line:84%
you know what would be
really great is film strips?

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:55.405 align:middle line:84%
Like, let's go big
on film strips.

00:30:55.405 --> 00:30:57.280 align:middle line:84%
You know what would
be really great is

00:30:57.280 --> 00:30:59.050 align:middle line:90%
closed circuit television.

00:30:59.050 --> 00:31:02.080 align:middle line:84%
Wouldn't that be a
useful thing to have?

00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:04.498 align:middle line:84%
Like, it's not the teachers,
it's not the superintendents,

00:31:04.498 --> 00:31:06.290 align:middle line:84%
it's not the people
who are in the schools.

00:31:06.290 --> 00:31:08.860 align:middle line:84%
It's like some community members
who are like, let's do it.

00:31:08.860 --> 00:31:12.241 align:middle line:90%


00:31:12.241 --> 00:31:13.780 align:middle line:84%
In the United States
today, there's

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:16.360 align:middle line:84%
probably two versions of
that is there are definitely

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:21.365 align:middle line:84%
school boards whose identity
is shaped around being

00:31:21.365 --> 00:31:23.990 align:middle line:84%
a cutting edge school in a bunch
of the affluent suburban towns

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:27.620 align:middle line:84%
around Boston, like
Wellesley, Newton,

00:31:27.620 --> 00:31:30.388 align:middle line:84%
they want to be thought
of as having really great,

00:31:30.388 --> 00:31:32.430 align:middle line:84%
cutting edge, forward
thinking schools, you know?

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:34.730 align:middle line:84%
Newton spent $200 million
or something like that

00:31:34.730 --> 00:31:36.800 align:middle line:84%
on the last high
school that they built.

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:39.680 align:middle line:84%
And so it can be a way to
become a school board member is

00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:43.707 align:middle line:84%
like, I'm going to help
the school system modernize

00:31:43.707 --> 00:31:45.540 align:middle line:84%
and embrace technology
and things like that.

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:46.832 align:middle line:90%
There can also be the opposite.

00:31:46.832 --> 00:31:48.680 align:middle line:84%
There can also be
people who come in.

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:51.470 align:middle line:84%
The most common is people
who work for the technology

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:55.700 align:middle line:84%
industry, who are like, you have
no idea how much surveillance

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:57.410 align:middle line:90%
is built into this technology.

00:31:57.410 --> 00:32:01.540 align:middle line:84%
All the time when I talk to
principals or superintendents

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:04.040 align:middle line:84%
who are being like, we're kind
of having a hard time helping

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:05.970 align:middle line:84%
parents understand why these
things would be useful.

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:08.180 align:middle line:84%
It's like someone who works
for Google or Microsoft

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:11.720 align:middle line:84%
or something like that
who knows the surveillance

00:32:11.720 --> 00:32:15.260 align:middle line:84%
capacity of these tools
and is concerned about it.

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:15.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:17.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I just kind
of have an anecdote.

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:18.480 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:18.820 align:middle line:90%
Please.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:20.903 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I used to work
in edtech and educational

00:32:20.903 --> 00:32:21.420 align:middle line:90%
publishing.

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:24.000 align:middle line:84%
I worked on mostly products
for the state of Florida

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:28.570 align:middle line:84%
and really showed how much power
some of these districts have.

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:30.700 align:middle line:84%
Because the school board
would make the decision,

00:32:30.700 --> 00:32:33.420 align:middle line:84%
there were several times where
one or two districts in Florida

00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:35.045 align:middle line:84%
would suddenly say
they need something,

00:32:35.045 --> 00:32:37.770 align:middle line:84%
and that would change the entire
roadmap for a product that's

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:38.800 align:middle line:90%
used nationally.

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:40.800 align:middle line:84%
So it's like you have a
couple people in Florida

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:42.520 align:middle line:84%
who are like, we
really need this thing.

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:45.030 align:middle line:84%
And then suddenly it's like,
kids in Arkansas are also.

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:47.720 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Are
learning different.

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:49.270 align:middle line:90%
This is definitely still true.

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:51.870 align:middle line:84%
It's breaking down
a little bit because

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:54.600 align:middle line:84%
of the capacity for a
web publishing and custom

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:55.420 align:middle line:90%
publication.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:57.270 align:middle line:84%
But for a big chunk
of what you might--

00:32:57.270 --> 00:32:59.740 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, the latter
third of the 20th century,

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:04.920 align:middle line:84%
the first 10 years of the
21st century, basically,

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:07.590 align:middle line:84%
publishers would publish
a US history book.

00:33:07.590 --> 00:33:10.950 align:middle line:84%
How do they decide what to
make of a US history book?

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:14.070 align:middle line:84%
You say, well, what are
the most important states

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:17.220 align:middle line:90%
to sell this history book in?

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:18.178 align:middle line:90%
Florida is one of them.

00:33:18.178 --> 00:33:19.178 align:middle line:90%
What are the other ones?

00:33:19.178 --> 00:33:20.010 align:middle line:90%
You can name them.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:21.904 align:middle line:90%
California.

00:33:21.904 --> 00:33:22.710 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: New York.

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:24.100 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Texas, New York.

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:25.958 align:middle line:84%
California, Texas,
New York, Florida.

00:33:25.958 --> 00:33:27.750 align:middle line:84%
Those are four of the
most important states

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:29.790 align:middle line:90%
just by population.

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:34.777 align:middle line:84%
So now you got to think
about the standards of--

00:33:34.777 --> 00:33:35.860 align:middle line:90%
every state has standards.

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:37.610 align:middle line:84%
The history standards
of those four states

00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:39.540 align:middle line:84%
are more important than
the history standards

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:41.790 align:middle line:90%
of Maine and North Dakota.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:45.833 align:middle line:84%
And as political things
come up around-- history

00:33:45.833 --> 00:33:47.500 align:middle line:84%
is where this happens
pretty frequently,

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:50.460 align:middle line:84%
but it happens in math and
reading and other things, too.

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:55.510 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, actually, I
used to do consulting

00:33:55.510 --> 00:33:57.250 align:middle line:84%
for publishers of
history textbooks,

00:33:57.250 --> 00:34:00.630 align:middle line:84%
and once I saw the
matrix where it

00:34:00.630 --> 00:34:04.800 align:middle line:84%
was like all the possible
topics along the columns

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:06.810 align:middle line:84%
and all of the
states along the rows

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:09.449 align:middle line:84%
and x's of where all
the topics get taught.

00:34:09.449 --> 00:34:11.340 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the other
thing is there'll

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:14.340 align:middle line:84%
be a huge group of
Polish immigrants

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:16.940 align:middle line:84%
that come to some
state and they won't

00:34:16.940 --> 00:34:18.960 align:middle line:84%
see Polish people represented
in their textbooks

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:20.449 align:middle line:84%
and so they'll be
like, we're only

00:34:20.449 --> 00:34:22.760 align:middle line:84%
going to adopt books that
talk about the importance

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:24.268 align:middle line:84%
of Polish Americans
in the history.

00:34:24.268 --> 00:34:26.310 align:middle line:84%
And the publishing companies
are like, all right,

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:27.560 align:middle line:90%
add the Polish to it.

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:29.370 align:middle line:84%
But then the Italians do it
and the Cambodians do it,

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:31.078 align:middle line:84%
everybody else do it,
and the textbooks--

00:34:31.078 --> 00:34:34.860 align:middle line:84%
that's why when you picked up
your 800 pound history textbook,

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:38.094 align:middle line:84%
it was full of all these
weird little bits and pieces

00:34:38.094 --> 00:34:38.969 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:34:38.969 --> 00:34:40.550 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, these state level--

00:34:40.550 --> 00:34:42.420 align:middle line:84%
I mean, here's another
tiny feature of this,

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:43.880 align:middle line:90%
but it ends up being important.

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:46.830 align:middle line:84%
There are some states,
mostly in the South,

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:51.110 align:middle line:84%
where material adoptions happen
centrally or have some kind

00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:52.610 align:middle line:90%
of centralized function.

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:55.520 align:middle line:84%
So Texas, Florida, they
have state level boards

00:34:55.520 --> 00:34:58.860 align:middle line:84%
that say you can only adopt
books that pass these boards.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:02.228 align:middle line:84%
Massachusetts is a huge
local control state.

00:35:02.228 --> 00:35:03.770 align:middle line:84%
The school districts
in Massachusetts

00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:05.355 align:middle line:84%
will go bonkers
if the state tried

00:35:05.355 --> 00:35:07.730 align:middle line:84%
to tell them which textbooks
they could or could not use.

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:09.355 align:middle line:84%
There would be like
another revolution,

00:35:09.355 --> 00:35:13.100 align:middle line:84%
would be one if by land,
two if by C class Mercedes

00:35:13.100 --> 00:35:17.700 align:middle line:84%
or whatever it is, and they
just wouldn't allow it.

00:35:17.700 --> 00:35:19.640 align:middle line:84%
But it means that
those states that

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:21.740 align:middle line:84%
have of centralized
function actually

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:24.282 align:middle line:84%
end up having more influence
over the publishers because they

00:35:24.282 --> 00:35:25.948 align:middle line:84%
say, look, you want
to sell a book here?

00:35:25.948 --> 00:35:27.500 align:middle line:84%
And now it's not
just a book, now

00:35:27.500 --> 00:35:29.130 align:middle line:84%
it's a web program
or things like that.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.430 align:middle line:84%
Now the thing that
is breaking away

00:35:31.430 --> 00:35:35.960 align:middle line:84%
some of that monopoly, although
also causing problems, is states

00:35:35.960 --> 00:35:40.010 align:middle line:84%
can say, OK, we're going
to pass all these laws that

00:35:40.010 --> 00:35:41.960 align:middle line:84%
have the effect of
way ramping down

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:43.790 align:middle line:90%
the teaching of Black history.

00:35:43.790 --> 00:35:46.200 align:middle line:84%
And publishers can
be like, all right,

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:47.780 align:middle line:84%
well, your web
version of that, we'll

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:49.405 align:middle line:84%
delete this and this
and this and this.

00:35:49.405 --> 00:35:51.705 align:middle line:84%
But over in New York or
California and liberal states,

00:35:51.705 --> 00:35:53.580 align:middle line:84%
you can have all that
kind of stuff in there.

00:35:53.580 --> 00:35:54.390 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Khalil?

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:56.600 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:35:56.600 --> 00:35:59.150 align:middle line:90%
Kind of going off of that.

00:35:59.150 --> 00:36:02.240 align:middle line:84%
The political platforms
play a large role

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:03.440 align:middle line:90%
in most of these places.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:06.000 align:middle line:84%
In the US, that's
very much there.

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:11.070 align:middle line:84%
But as an example from my own
country, at every public school,

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:15.070 align:middle line:84%
they have a speaker system
at the start of the school,

00:36:15.070 --> 00:36:18.100 align:middle line:84%
specifically because they want
to play the National Anthem.

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:24.000 align:middle line:84%
That's something they did really
early, the kids have to do it.

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:26.880 align:middle line:84%
To oh, there's so
much like technology

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:28.450 align:middle line:90%
that's just one thing.

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:30.600 align:middle line:90%
Why is this the only piece of--

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:32.910 align:middle line:84%
there is some kind
of incentive they

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:37.410 align:middle line:84%
want to build with
political motives.

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:40.290 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And then
does that broadcast system

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:43.500 align:middle line:84%
be used at other parts
of the day as well, or?

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:45.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Only gets used
at the start of the school

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:49.710 align:middle line:84%
where the National
Anthem and some kids--

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:52.770 align:middle line:84%
every classroom does a
session where they just

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:59.310 align:middle line:84%
do some religious stuff,
some Arabic poems, some fun

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:01.070 align:middle line:90%
facts, and then just that.

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:02.070 align:middle line:90%
But that's mostly just--

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:03.153 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: I love that.

00:37:03.153 --> 00:37:05.640 align:middle line:84%
I think that's a
fabulous example.

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:07.705 align:middle line:84%
Whatever the state
government is--

00:37:07.705 --> 00:37:08.830 align:middle line:90%
the state is in the nation.

00:37:08.830 --> 00:37:12.660 align:middle line:84%
The nation state government
says every school must broadcast

00:37:12.660 --> 00:37:14.910 align:middle line:90%
the National Anthem every day.

00:37:14.910 --> 00:37:16.470 align:middle line:84%
Every single school
in the country

00:37:16.470 --> 00:37:19.560 align:middle line:84%
then has to build a
technology infrastructure that

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:20.590 align:middle line:90%
allows that to happen.

00:37:20.590 --> 00:37:22.990 align:middle line:84%
And then once that technology
infrastructure is there,

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:26.250 align:middle line:84%
it can be used in other kinds
of ways as well or not used

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:27.930 align:middle line:84%
or other kinds of
things like that.

00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:32.070 align:middle line:84%
You can find national examples
of this in other kinds of ways.

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:34.530 align:middle line:84%
One of the most interesting
countries right now

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:37.950 align:middle line:84%
for education policymakers
to watch is Estonia.

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:40.560 align:middle line:84%
About every decade, some country
that people aren't really

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:44.200 align:middle line:84%
paying attention to gets really
good international test scores.

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:45.835 align:middle line:90%
So Singapore was it for a while.

00:37:45.835 --> 00:37:46.960 align:middle line:90%
Finland was it for a while.

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:48.877 align:middle line:84%
People were obsessed
with Finland for a while.

00:37:48.877 --> 00:37:49.960 align:middle line:90%
Estonia is kind of last.

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:51.480 align:middle line:84%
Who are you people
and why-- like,

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:53.400 align:middle line:90%
why are you so good at reading?

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:56.910 align:middle line:90%
And Estonia is very famous for--

00:37:56.910 --> 00:37:58.710 align:middle line:90%
sometimes it's called E-stonia.

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:01.110 align:middle line:84%
Their government has really
embraced the internet

00:38:01.110 --> 00:38:02.882 align:middle line:84%
in a very centralized
kind of way,

00:38:02.882 --> 00:38:04.590 align:middle line:84%
and so people are
looking at and thinking

00:38:04.590 --> 00:38:07.170 align:middle line:84%
about how education technologies
are adopted in Estonia

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:08.170 align:middle line:90%
and other kinds of ways.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:10.590 align:middle line:84%
But it wasn't a policy that
was just for the schools.

00:38:10.590 --> 00:38:13.010 align:middle line:84%
They were like, we're going
to put Estonia on the map

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:15.750 align:middle line:84%
by having really good
digital government services.

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:17.570 align:middle line:84%
And that national
policy decision

00:38:17.570 --> 00:38:20.150 align:middle line:84%
ends up affecting the learning
experiences of kids in schools

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:21.330 align:middle line:90%
and all kinds of places.

00:38:21.330 --> 00:38:24.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a great example.

00:38:24.230 --> 00:38:26.472 align:middle line:90%
Students.

00:38:26.472 --> 00:38:30.175 align:middle line:84%
What are the interests and
disincentives of students?

00:38:30.175 --> 00:38:33.457 align:middle line:90%


00:38:33.457 --> 00:38:34.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's cool.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:35.998 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's a new
piece of technology.

00:38:35.998 --> 00:38:38.000 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: So almost
all of us like novelty.

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:42.980 align:middle line:84%
That is one of the advantages
that technology has.

00:38:42.980 --> 00:38:45.350 align:middle line:84%
Although an amazing
thing about novelty

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:48.470 align:middle line:84%
is how shockingly
quickly it wears off.

00:38:48.470 --> 00:38:50.750 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I was reading this
on Reddit the other day.

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:53.180 align:middle line:84%
A teacher was
being like, I don't

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:55.920 align:middle line:84%
know, like I brought a
Nintendo Switch into class

00:38:55.920 --> 00:38:58.227 align:middle line:84%
so we could play these
mathematical logic games.

00:38:58.227 --> 00:39:00.060 align:middle line:84%
And my students were
like, oh, please, Miss,

00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:01.530 align:middle line:90%
can we just do worksheets?

00:39:01.530 --> 00:39:04.340 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, fine,
do worksheets then.

00:39:04.340 --> 00:39:07.890 align:middle line:84%
But they're like, oh, man, you
want to play some online game?

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:08.990 align:middle line:90%
I don't want to play--

00:39:08.990 --> 00:39:10.850 align:middle line:84%
now, there would have been some
moment that this class would

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:11.700 align:middle line:90%
be like, what?

00:39:11.700 --> 00:39:13.290 align:middle line:84%
We can play a computer
game in class?

00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:13.957 align:middle line:90%
This is amazing.

00:39:13.957 --> 00:39:17.192 align:middle line:90%


00:39:17.192 --> 00:39:19.820 align:middle line:84%
It's an interesting feature of
education, technology adoption

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:22.790 align:middle line:84%
that novelty, can be
extremely compelling

00:39:22.790 --> 00:39:25.730 align:middle line:84%
for a short period of time and
then wear off pretty quickly.

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:27.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you
have any comment

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:32.990 align:middle line:84%
on why students default is now
back to that pen and paper?

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:34.640 align:middle line:84%
Because I mean, people
are always like,

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:37.680 align:middle line:84%
education has been
the same for decades.

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:39.200 align:middle line:84%
And why does it
always look this way

00:39:39.200 --> 00:39:40.680 align:middle line:90%
even after we've reformed it?

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:43.610 align:middle line:84%
Like, why is the default
that pen and paper?

00:39:43.610 --> 00:39:44.540 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:39:44.540 --> 00:39:47.060 align:middle line:84%
So, well, let's just
highlight an important thing

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:48.950 align:middle line:84%
that you observed,
which we were talking

00:39:48.950 --> 00:39:51.770 align:middle line:84%
about over here too, which is
just schools are incredibly

00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:54.720 align:middle line:90%
conservative institutions.

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:57.860 align:middle line:84%
Why would that-- small C
conservative, not right wing.

00:39:57.860 --> 00:39:59.000 align:middle line:90%
Some of them are, but.

00:39:59.000 --> 00:40:00.210 align:middle line:90%
They're small C conservative.

00:40:00.210 --> 00:40:02.413 align:middle line:84%
They do things the same
way over and over again.

00:40:02.413 --> 00:40:04.080 align:middle line:84%
What are some possible
reasons for that?

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:06.827 align:middle line:84%
One is, if you went and
interviewed parents and said--

00:40:06.827 --> 00:40:08.910 align:middle line:84%
who are another stakeholder
that a bunch of people

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:11.370 align:middle line:84%
brought up-- how much do
you want us experimenting

00:40:11.370 --> 00:40:12.690 align:middle line:90%
with your children?

00:40:12.690 --> 00:40:15.240 align:middle line:84%
The answer to that is
going to be pretty low.

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:17.880 align:middle line:84%
The enthusiasm that people have
for experimenting on children

00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:18.780 align:middle line:90%
is low.

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:22.200 align:middle line:84%
Second, everyone in
the educational system

00:40:22.200 --> 00:40:23.880 align:middle line:84%
who's an adult has
what's been sometimes

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:26.340 align:middle line:84%
called the apprenticeship
of observation.

00:40:26.340 --> 00:40:28.080 align:middle line:84%
You all have been
in school somewhere

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:32.218 align:middle line:84%
between 13 and 18 years, and
that shapes your experience.

00:40:32.218 --> 00:40:34.260 align:middle line:84%
And so people who have
roughly the same education

00:40:34.260 --> 00:40:36.820 align:middle line:84%
level as you go into
schools and become teachers,

00:40:36.820 --> 00:40:40.590 align:middle line:84%
and they bring that 13, 18, 20
years of educational experience

00:40:40.590 --> 00:40:42.960 align:middle line:84%
with them, and that
shapes the way you

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:44.730 align:middle line:90%
think schools should unfold.

00:40:44.730 --> 00:40:47.298 align:middle line:84%
Parents can bring
those expectations in.

00:40:47.298 --> 00:40:48.340 align:middle line:90%
I will never forget this.

00:40:48.340 --> 00:40:49.387 align:middle line:90%
I was once working--

00:40:49.387 --> 00:40:50.970 align:middle line:84%
I was conversing
with a school in Utah

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:54.100 align:middle line:84%
that was trying to switch to
openly licensed materials.

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:56.310 align:middle line:84%
It was, I don't know, 15
years ago, 18 years ago,

00:40:56.310 --> 00:40:56.890 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:40:56.890 --> 00:40:58.682 align:middle line:84%
They were trying to
get away from textbooks

00:40:58.682 --> 00:41:01.500 align:middle line:84%
and to have people use digital
materials, particularly

00:41:01.500 --> 00:41:04.242 align:middle line:84%
digital materials that their
teachers could edit so that they

00:41:04.242 --> 00:41:06.450 align:middle line:84%
could make them tailored to
their students and things

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:08.370 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:10.190 align:middle line:84%
But they still kept
textbooks in classroom,

00:41:10.190 --> 00:41:12.190 align:middle line:84%
and I can't tell you
whether or not that's true.

00:41:12.190 --> 00:41:14.760 align:middle line:84%
But he says the reason why we
keep textbooks in classrooms

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:16.750 align:middle line:84%
is that there aren't
textbooks in classrooms,

00:41:16.750 --> 00:41:19.175 align:middle line:90%
parents will be upset.

00:41:19.175 --> 00:41:20.800 align:middle line:84%
Parents, when they
go into a classroom,

00:41:20.800 --> 00:41:22.050 align:middle line:90%
expect to see textbooks there.

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:25.537 align:middle line:84%
Basically, it was cheaper to
keep buying enough textbooks

00:41:25.537 --> 00:41:27.120 align:middle line:84%
to be in all of the
classrooms than it

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:29.730 align:middle line:84%
was to hire administrators
to explain to parents

00:41:29.730 --> 00:41:32.700 align:middle line:84%
why there weren't textbooks
in the classroom anymore.

00:41:32.700 --> 00:41:35.430 align:middle line:84%
So all of these
different stakeholders,

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:40.890 align:middle line:84%
this conservative vision of
education is part of that.

00:41:40.890 --> 00:41:44.160 align:middle line:84%
One possibility is
that that's really bad,

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:46.800 align:middle line:84%
that there are actually
way better ways of doing

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:48.100 align:middle line:90%
things that are out there.

00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:50.070 align:middle line:84%
And it's a bummer that
we're so conservative

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:52.140 align:middle line:84%
and that's really
holding us back.

00:41:52.140 --> 00:41:55.950 align:middle line:84%
Another possibility is that the
practices that exist in schools

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:58.900 align:middle line:84%
have been refined over
decades and centuries,

00:41:58.900 --> 00:42:01.740 align:middle line:84%
and they are the way they are
because they're pretty darn

00:42:01.740 --> 00:42:03.675 align:middle line:90%
good.

00:42:03.675 --> 00:42:07.010 align:middle line:84%
That we keep doing certain kinds
of things because they work.

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:09.445 align:middle line:84%
So you'll have to
figure out for yourself,

00:42:09.445 --> 00:42:12.920 align:middle line:84%
if you hear examples of
students adopting old practices,

00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:14.350 align:middle line:84%
are they adopting
those practices

00:42:14.350 --> 00:42:16.725 align:middle line:84%
because they work really well
and because they like them,

00:42:16.725 --> 00:42:18.760 align:middle line:84%
because they're
comfortable and familiar?

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:22.130 align:middle line:84%
There's an argument that,
particularly in our age,

00:42:22.130 --> 00:42:26.380 align:middle line:84%
where students are fixated on
grades and performance in a way

00:42:26.380 --> 00:42:29.065 align:middle line:84%
that they might have not been
in decades past because--

00:42:29.065 --> 00:42:31.550 align:middle line:84%
not because kids are more
materialistic or something.

00:42:31.550 --> 00:42:33.220 align:middle line:84%
It's because of
economic uncertainty,

00:42:33.220 --> 00:42:35.530 align:middle line:84%
because as the gap between
wealthy and poor people

00:42:35.530 --> 00:42:37.790 align:middle line:84%
widens and the stakes of
education become higher,

00:42:37.790 --> 00:42:39.760 align:middle line:84%
people become more
fixated on it.

00:42:39.760 --> 00:42:42.550 align:middle line:84%
And so those kids who are
adopting familiar matters,

00:42:42.550 --> 00:42:44.160 align:middle line:84%
do they really like
the familiarity?

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:46.660 align:middle line:84%
Or do they know how to do well
in the familiarity and things

00:42:46.660 --> 00:42:47.160 align:middle line:90%
like that?

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:50.350 align:middle line:84%
But again, keep
tying all the things

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:53.860 align:middle line:84%
that we were talking about back
to the decision of educators

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:57.490 align:middle line:84%
trying to implement
new technologies.

00:42:57.490 --> 00:42:59.800 align:middle line:84%
Everyone in your
system is going to have

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:01.870 align:middle line:84%
some kind of
conservative impulse

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:03.210 align:middle line:90%
all the way down to students.

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:04.627 align:middle line:84%
That conservative
impulse might be

00:43:04.627 --> 00:43:07.390 align:middle line:84%
balanced by the novelty effect
that we're talking about,

00:43:07.390 --> 00:43:08.140 align:middle line:90%
but it's in there.

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:10.830 align:middle line:84%
So if you want a new
technology to really

00:43:10.830 --> 00:43:13.240 align:middle line:84%
be adopted in some
kind of meaningful way,

00:43:13.240 --> 00:43:15.780 align:middle line:84%
one strategy you can
have is to have it

00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:18.010 align:middle line:90%
align with existing practices.

00:43:18.010 --> 00:43:19.767 align:middle line:84%
You could say, let's
take technologies

00:43:19.767 --> 00:43:21.600 align:middle line:84%
that do the kinds of
things that are already

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:22.590 align:middle line:90%
happening in schools.

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:25.210 align:middle line:84%
That will be less upsetting,
that will require less training.

00:43:25.210 --> 00:43:26.418 align:middle line:90%
That will require less upset.

00:43:26.418 --> 00:43:28.362 align:middle line:84%
It probably also
won't help that much.

00:43:28.362 --> 00:43:30.820 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the people selling the
technologies won't say that.

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:33.973 align:middle line:84%
But if you're just digitizing
existing practices, how likely

00:43:33.973 --> 00:43:36.390 align:middle line:84%
are you to have substantial
gains from digitizing existing

00:43:36.390 --> 00:43:37.000 align:middle line:90%
practices?

00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:38.753 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe you're a big,
ambitious reformer

00:43:38.753 --> 00:43:41.170 align:middle line:84%
and you say, no, no, no, we
could be doing so much better.

00:43:41.170 --> 00:43:44.020 align:middle line:84%
We could go from 2% to
100% efficiency in schools.

00:43:44.020 --> 00:43:46.450 align:middle line:84%
We just have to make big
changes in our practice.

00:43:46.450 --> 00:43:48.210 align:middle line:84%
Well, making big
changes in practice

00:43:48.210 --> 00:43:52.425 align:middle line:84%
requires convincing people to
change all of these things.

00:43:52.425 --> 00:43:54.820 align:middle line:84%
I've read this book
a zillion times now,

00:43:54.820 --> 00:44:00.065 align:middle line:84%
but I was just revisiting a
line in here that I loved.

00:44:00.065 --> 00:44:01.440 align:middle line:84%
He said something
towards the end

00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:06.733 align:middle line:84%
there where settings have
plans for their inhabitants.

00:44:06.733 --> 00:44:07.650 align:middle line:90%
Anybody remember that?

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:10.680 align:middle line:84%
He was talking about the
physical layout of a classroom,

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:13.540 align:middle line:84%
but he was using a broader
sociological principle.

00:44:13.540 --> 00:44:16.290 align:middle line:84%
Settings have plans
for their inhabitants.

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:19.440 align:middle line:84%
People interact in a
space in a certain way

00:44:19.440 --> 00:44:23.203 align:middle line:84%
because the physical space is
laid out and built in that way.

00:44:23.203 --> 00:44:25.620 align:middle line:84%
So I was talking with these
guys about one of the problems

00:44:25.620 --> 00:44:26.790 align:middle line:90%
that filmstrips have.

00:44:26.790 --> 00:44:28.930 align:middle line:84%
To have a filmstrip
in your classroom,

00:44:28.930 --> 00:44:31.360 align:middle line:84%
you have to take a giant
projector on wheels,

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:33.280 align:middle line:84%
and you have to roll
it into the space.

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:37.260 align:middle line:84%
There would have been some
classrooms filled with early

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:40.440 align:middle line:84%
20th century urban schools
with tons of immigrants who are

00:44:40.440 --> 00:44:43.080 align:middle line:84%
pouring in on America's
shores that don't have room

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:45.060 align:middle line:84%
in their classroom
for a big thing.

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:48.000 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that filmstrips
had that was a huge pain

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:49.810 align:middle line:90%
is these giant film reels.

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:52.450 align:middle line:84%
The early film reels
were really, really big.

00:44:52.450 --> 00:44:54.395 align:middle line:84%
Where are you going
to put these things?

00:44:54.395 --> 00:44:56.700 align:middle line:84%
Your town did not
build a building

00:44:56.700 --> 00:45:02.070 align:middle line:84%
that had an archival film studio
storage unit attached to it.

00:45:02.070 --> 00:45:04.830 align:middle line:84%
Let's say you have a big school
and there are four first grade

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:07.052 align:middle line:84%
classes, and you want to
buy a filmstrip for all four

00:45:07.052 --> 00:45:09.010 align:middle line:84%
of those first-- like,
are you going to build--

00:45:09.010 --> 00:45:11.580 align:middle line:84%
are you going to buy four
copies of the same filmstrip?

00:45:11.580 --> 00:45:13.900 align:middle line:84%
Is each classroom going to
have its own film library?

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:15.550 align:middle line:84%
If it has some
centralized library,

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:19.090 align:middle line:84%
you have to have some way
of tagging, archiving,

00:45:19.090 --> 00:45:21.340 align:middle line:84%
maintaining those things,
bringing it into classrooms.

00:45:21.340 --> 00:45:24.270 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are contemporary
versions of all of these things.

00:45:24.270 --> 00:45:26.460 align:middle line:84%
Oh, you want to give
every kid a device?

00:45:26.460 --> 00:45:28.960 align:middle line:84%
How is that kid's device
going to get on the network?

00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:30.670 align:middle line:84%
How is he going to
be able to log in?

00:45:30.670 --> 00:45:32.860 align:middle line:84%
Who's going to deal when
they lose their password?

00:45:32.860 --> 00:45:35.580 align:middle line:84%
What's going to happen
when their devices break?

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:38.470 align:middle line:84%
Are you going to make families
pay for the devices they break?

00:45:38.470 --> 00:45:39.580 align:middle line:90%
Your kids are super poor.

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:40.570 align:middle line:90%
They can't afford that.

00:45:40.570 --> 00:45:42.820 align:middle line:84%
So you're going to contract
with an insurance company.

00:45:42.820 --> 00:45:44.730 align:middle line:84%
Who, on your staff,
has experience

00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:46.590 align:middle line:84%
contracting with
insurance companies

00:45:46.590 --> 00:45:50.338 align:middle line:90%
to insure computing devices?

00:45:50.338 --> 00:45:51.880 align:middle line:84%
Like, you didn't
learn that at your--

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:53.340 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we'll talk with
the education school

00:45:53.340 --> 00:45:55.465 align:middle line:84%
deans on Wednesday, but
I'm pretty sure they're not

00:45:55.465 --> 00:45:59.470 align:middle line:84%
teaching their students how
to manage insurance contracts

00:45:59.470 --> 00:46:01.450 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:46:01.450 --> 00:46:05.470 align:middle line:90%
All of those things become--

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:08.740 align:middle line:84%
well, they have a combination
of incentives and disincentives

00:46:08.740 --> 00:46:10.630 align:middle line:90%
that are associated with them.

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:11.745 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:46:11.745 --> 00:46:13.120 align:middle line:84%
Any of these other
people that it

00:46:13.120 --> 00:46:16.750 align:middle line:84%
seems like it's important
for us to touch on before we

00:46:16.750 --> 00:46:18.200 align:middle line:90%
come to the conclusion?

00:46:18.200 --> 00:46:20.650 align:middle line:90%
We talked about parents.

00:46:20.650 --> 00:46:22.670 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I don't know
how much we talked.

00:46:22.670 --> 00:46:25.040 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's one last thing
that we could talk about.

00:46:25.040 --> 00:46:27.730 align:middle line:84%
We talked a little bit
about school administrators.

00:46:27.730 --> 00:46:34.960 align:middle line:84%
But a thing to be particularly
attentive to in schools

00:46:34.960 --> 00:46:37.690 align:middle line:84%
is just how many different kinds
of adults with different roles

00:46:37.690 --> 00:46:38.190 align:middle line:90%
there are.

00:46:38.190 --> 00:46:39.700 align:middle line:90%
So we talked about teachers.

00:46:39.700 --> 00:46:42.170 align:middle line:84%
There are people who
are school principals.

00:46:42.170 --> 00:46:43.910 align:middle line:84%
Like if you're a
school principal,

00:46:43.910 --> 00:46:46.240 align:middle line:84%
what are the most
urgent concerns that you

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:48.790 align:middle line:90%
have on any given day?

00:46:48.790 --> 00:46:50.260 align:middle line:84%
Like, what are the
main incentives

00:46:50.260 --> 00:46:51.903 align:middle line:90%
you have as a school principal?

00:46:51.903 --> 00:46:52.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Your budget.

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:53.230 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yep.

00:46:53.230 --> 00:46:54.830 align:middle line:84%
You got to make sure you
can pay for everything.

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:56.150 align:middle line:84%
A teacher is not
worried about that.

00:46:56.150 --> 00:46:57.370 align:middle line:90%
Teacher will buy as much--

00:46:57.370 --> 00:46:58.320 align:middle line:84%
if the school will
pay paid for it,

00:46:58.320 --> 00:46:59.780 align:middle line:84%
they'll take as much
technology as they want.

00:46:59.780 --> 00:47:00.530 align:middle line:90%
Some of them.

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:02.480 align:middle line:84%
But principals have
to balance the budget.

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:05.070 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the weird thing
is in the United States,

00:47:05.070 --> 00:47:06.817 align:middle line:84%
it may not at all be
a principal's role

00:47:06.817 --> 00:47:07.650 align:middle line:90%
to balance a budget.

00:47:07.650 --> 00:47:09.553 align:middle line:84%
It may be entirely
a district role.

00:47:09.553 --> 00:47:10.970 align:middle line:84%
So there are some
school districts

00:47:10.970 --> 00:47:13.500 align:middle line:84%
where the district will make
all the purchasing decisions.

00:47:13.500 --> 00:47:15.020 align:middle line:84%
There are other districts
where the districts will

00:47:15.020 --> 00:47:17.478 align:middle line:84%
take big chunks of money, send
it down to schools, and say,

00:47:17.478 --> 00:47:21.230 align:middle line:84%
schools, make all the
purchasing decisions.

00:47:21.230 --> 00:47:23.815 align:middle line:84%
What else is super
important for principals?

00:47:23.815 --> 00:47:25.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was
going to say just

00:47:25.190 --> 00:47:28.020 align:middle line:84%
maintaining the school building
itself for student safety.

00:47:28.020 --> 00:47:28.310 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:47:28.310 --> 00:47:28.810 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:47:28.810 --> 00:47:31.370 align:middle line:84%
Student safety, maintaining
the physical school building,

00:47:31.370 --> 00:47:33.620 align:middle line:84%
making sure things are running
and operating and stuff

00:47:33.620 --> 00:47:35.017 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:47:35.017 --> 00:47:37.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are they also
managing the relationships

00:47:37.100 --> 00:47:38.840 align:middle line:84%
with the district
and also parents?

00:47:38.840 --> 00:47:39.960 align:middle line:90%
Like, all these outside--

00:47:39.960 --> 00:47:42.380 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yep, yep.

00:47:42.380 --> 00:47:45.230 align:middle line:84%
And principals
are quintessential

00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:47.600 align:middle line:84%
organizational behavior
middle managers.

00:47:47.600 --> 00:47:50.040 align:middle line:84%
They're empowered to do
some things but not others.

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:52.665 align:middle line:84%
They're accountable to lots of
different kinds of stakeholders.

00:47:52.665 --> 00:47:55.910 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds of complexity
in what they're doing.

00:47:55.910 --> 00:47:57.910 align:middle line:90%
A lot of them are pretty keyed--

00:47:57.910 --> 00:48:00.150 align:middle line:84%
or evaluated based on
test scores, especially

00:48:00.150 --> 00:48:04.182 align:middle line:84%
in places where students are
at risk of not passing tests.

00:48:04.182 --> 00:48:05.890 align:middle line:84%
Depending upon the
policy infrastructure,

00:48:05.890 --> 00:48:07.770 align:middle line:84%
test scores really
matter to them.

00:48:07.770 --> 00:48:12.150 align:middle line:84%
Safety and the orderly
operation of schools.

00:48:12.150 --> 00:48:14.890 align:middle line:84%
Every person in the building
wants schools to be orderly,

00:48:14.890 --> 00:48:16.860 align:middle line:84%
but when schools are
disorderly, the person who

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:19.110 align:middle line:84%
has to deal with it the most
is an assistant principal

00:48:19.110 --> 00:48:19.818 align:middle line:90%
or the principal.

00:48:19.818 --> 00:48:22.360 align:middle line:90%
It's just more work for them.

00:48:22.360 --> 00:48:26.040 align:middle line:84%
You let all the kids have
phones and fights break out

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:27.332 align:middle line:90%
and all kinds of things happen.

00:48:27.332 --> 00:48:29.665 align:middle line:84%
Teachers have to deal with
it, but the principals really

00:48:29.665 --> 00:48:30.660 align:middle line:90%
have to deal with it.

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:31.970 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I was just like--

00:48:31.970 --> 00:48:33.060 align:middle line:90%
really short comment.

00:48:33.060 --> 00:48:35.590 align:middle line:84%
Just, when you asked us,
what does a principal do?

00:48:35.590 --> 00:48:37.210 align:middle line:84%
I've been around
them my entire life,

00:48:37.210 --> 00:48:39.870 align:middle line:84%
and I was like, I can only think
of like two or three things

00:48:39.870 --> 00:48:41.920 align:middle line:84%
that I know a principal
actually does.

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:44.730 align:middle line:84%
And I just think it's really
strange that most people aren't

00:48:44.730 --> 00:48:46.530 align:middle line:84%
sitting you down
and being like, this

00:48:46.530 --> 00:48:49.473 align:middle line:84%
is the person you go to
for x, y, and z things--

00:48:49.473 --> 00:48:50.265 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:48:50.265 --> 00:48:51.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --around
these organizations.

00:48:51.890 --> 00:48:53.215 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:48:53.215 --> 00:48:54.840 align:middle line:84%
I think there are a
few things that you

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:56.190 align:middle line:90%
can grasp from that insight.

00:48:56.190 --> 00:49:00.300 align:middle line:84%
First of all, schools are
just unbelievably complicated.

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:02.610 align:middle line:84%
I've spent the last
20 years basically

00:49:02.610 --> 00:49:04.450 align:middle line:84%
doing nothing but
thinking about schools,

00:49:04.450 --> 00:49:06.242 align:middle line:84%
and I still don't
understand all the things

00:49:06.242 --> 00:49:08.870 align:middle line:90%
that are going on in there.

00:49:08.870 --> 00:49:11.220 align:middle line:84%
Just as an ecosystem,
they're unbelievably

00:49:11.220 --> 00:49:12.840 align:middle line:90%
complex and fascinating.

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:16.780 align:middle line:84%
The second is as you transition,
if you decide to work,

00:49:16.780 --> 00:49:18.870 align:middle line:84%
be involved in
education, you have

00:49:18.870 --> 00:49:22.170 align:middle line:84%
to relearn all this stuff going
from a student to being someone

00:49:22.170 --> 00:49:23.320 align:middle line:90%
who's working in education.

00:49:23.320 --> 00:49:25.750 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds of things
that were hidden from you,

00:49:25.750 --> 00:49:27.833 align:middle line:84%
masked from you, or just
weren't important to you.

00:49:27.833 --> 00:49:31.020 align:middle line:84%
The odds that any of you
were real troublemakers

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:33.570 align:middle line:84%
is pretty low, given
that you're at MIT.

00:49:33.570 --> 00:49:37.350 align:middle line:84%
But there's a classroom full
of people sitting somewhere

00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:39.870 align:middle line:84%
that knew principles pretty
intimately because they spent

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:43.240 align:middle line:84%
too much time in their office
because of things that they did

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:43.840 align:middle line:90%
and so forth.

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:44.140 align:middle line:90%
So good.

00:49:44.140 --> 00:49:44.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:47.280 align:middle line:84%
If part of what happens
to you in this class

00:49:47.280 --> 00:49:49.390 align:middle line:84%
is a lot of times
in social science,

00:49:49.390 --> 00:49:51.438 align:middle line:84%
we talk about making
the familiar unfamiliar.

00:49:51.438 --> 00:49:53.980 align:middle line:84%
A thing that you've looked at
your whole life and being like,

00:49:53.980 --> 00:49:55.180 align:middle line:84%
whoa, look at all
these things there

00:49:55.180 --> 00:49:56.555 align:middle line:84%
that I wasn't
paying attention to

00:49:56.555 --> 00:49:59.770 align:middle line:84%
or wasn't made aware to me
and those kinds of things.

00:49:59.770 --> 00:50:05.290 align:middle line:84%
Maybe here's one
very concrete thing.

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:09.160 align:middle line:84%
Let's think of something
like a student information

00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:10.582 align:middle line:90%
system or Canvas.

00:50:10.582 --> 00:50:13.040 align:middle line:84%
Let's think of something like
a learning management system.

00:50:13.040 --> 00:50:16.690 align:middle line:84%
Who spends the most
time using Canvas?

00:50:16.690 --> 00:50:18.598 align:middle line:84%
Tell me about the
people who use Canvas.

00:50:18.598 --> 00:50:19.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Students.

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:20.080 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Students.

00:50:20.080 --> 00:50:21.790 align:middle line:84%
Students spend a
lot of hours logged

00:50:21.790 --> 00:50:23.090 align:middle line:90%
in looking at stuff on Canvas.

00:50:23.090 --> 00:50:24.928 align:middle line:90%
Who else looks at Canvas?

00:50:24.928 --> 00:50:25.720 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Teachers.

00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:26.678 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Teachers.

00:50:26.678 --> 00:50:28.340 align:middle line:90%
Who buys Canvas?

00:50:28.340 --> 00:50:29.800 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Not the students.

00:50:29.800 --> 00:50:31.600 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Not
students and teachers.

00:50:31.600 --> 00:50:34.675 align:middle line:90%


00:50:34.675 --> 00:50:35.690 align:middle line:90%
In business school.

00:50:35.690 --> 00:50:38.770 align:middle line:84%
I think they would call this
a third party payer problem.

00:50:38.770 --> 00:50:41.890 align:middle line:84%
So if you are a company that's
making a learning management

00:50:41.890 --> 00:50:48.040 align:middle line:84%
system, of your
stakeholders, who

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:51.670 align:middle line:84%
has the most power to determine
whether or not your thing gets

00:50:51.670 --> 00:50:53.870 align:middle line:90%
purchased?

00:50:53.870 --> 00:50:56.240 align:middle line:84%
Some director of education
technology or something

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:57.630 align:middle line:90%
like that, some IT person.

00:50:57.630 --> 00:51:01.640 align:middle line:84%
So if you've ever wondered,
why is this so terrible?

00:51:01.640 --> 00:51:04.325 align:middle line:90%
It wasn't built for you.

00:51:04.325 --> 00:51:07.170 align:middle line:84%
In some important ways, it was
literally not built for you.

00:51:07.170 --> 00:51:10.910 align:middle line:84%
It was built to solve the
problems of some middle level

00:51:10.910 --> 00:51:12.980 align:middle line:84%
information technology
manager who's going

00:51:12.980 --> 00:51:14.130 align:middle line:90%
to buy one of those things.

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:17.515 align:middle line:84%
So third party payer
problems are rife across--

00:51:17.515 --> 00:51:19.640 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the problem with
third party payer problems

00:51:19.640 --> 00:51:21.307 align:middle line:84%
from a classical
economics point of view

00:51:21.307 --> 00:51:24.521 align:middle line:84%
is you just have
misaligned incentives.

00:51:24.521 --> 00:51:27.650 align:middle line:84%
In some respects, you could
say that improving the teaching

00:51:27.650 --> 00:51:30.740 align:middle line:84%
and learning experience
on Canvas is a cost.

00:51:30.740 --> 00:51:33.125 align:middle line:84%
It's something that
reduces your profit.

00:51:33.125 --> 00:51:35.000 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, you have to
make it good enough so

00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:37.588 align:middle line:84%
that if enough teachers and
stuff enrolled-- any of you

00:51:37.588 --> 00:51:38.755 align:middle line:90%
ever have to use Blackboard?

00:51:38.755 --> 00:51:39.770 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes, I have.

00:51:39.770 --> 00:51:41.270 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK, good.

00:51:41.270 --> 00:51:43.170 align:middle line:84%
I once met the guy
who made Blackboard,

00:51:43.170 --> 00:51:45.350 align:middle line:84%
and I was just standing
there in line behind him

00:51:45.350 --> 00:51:46.820 align:middle line:84%
or something like that,
being like, what would it

00:51:46.820 --> 00:51:49.670 align:middle line:84%
be like to have made a piece of
software that millions of people

00:51:49.670 --> 00:51:51.030 align:middle line:90%
hate?

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:53.220 align:middle line:84%
Like, what would it have
been like to introduce

00:51:53.220 --> 00:51:56.750 align:middle line:84%
so much unhappiness in the
world with what you've built?

00:51:56.750 --> 00:52:01.800 align:middle line:84%
I think it would have been rude,
I think, in the buffet line

00:52:01.800 --> 00:52:03.270 align:middle line:90%
to ask him that question.

00:52:03.270 --> 00:52:04.380 align:middle line:84%
Here's another thing
that I want to say

00:52:04.380 --> 00:52:05.530 align:middle line:90%
about who buys these things.

00:52:05.530 --> 00:52:06.810 align:middle line:84%
And this is really
important too,

00:52:06.810 --> 00:52:09.018 align:middle line:84%
especially to any of you
who are interested in either

00:52:09.018 --> 00:52:10.020 align:middle line:90%
in education--

00:52:10.020 --> 00:52:14.220 align:middle line:84%
starting an education startup
or other kinds of things.

00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:17.410 align:middle line:84%
Who in a school
can buy technology?

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:22.390 align:middle line:84%
What are some possible titles
of people who buy technology?

00:52:22.390 --> 00:52:23.640 align:middle line:90%
We've mentioned a few of them.

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:24.515 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Librarians.

00:52:24.515 --> 00:52:27.265 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: A librarian
could buy technology.

00:52:27.265 --> 00:52:28.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Department chair.

00:52:28.390 --> 00:52:30.640 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: A department
chair could buy technology.

00:52:30.640 --> 00:52:33.780 align:middle line:90%


00:52:33.780 --> 00:52:34.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Teacher.

00:52:34.530 --> 00:52:35.530 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: A teacher.

00:52:35.530 --> 00:52:37.018 align:middle line:84%
They are amongst
the least likely.

00:52:37.018 --> 00:52:39.060 align:middle line:84%
Actually, sometimes people
have made the argument

00:52:39.060 --> 00:52:41.100 align:middle line:84%
that a problem in
the education system

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:45.270 align:middle line:84%
is it's very difficult to have
a real bottom up marketplace

00:52:45.270 --> 00:52:47.580 align:middle line:90%
kind of effect.

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:52.060 align:middle line:84%
In most places, teachers have
very few discretionary dollars.

00:52:52.060 --> 00:52:55.310 align:middle line:84%
I mean, that's why they're
holding bake sales and asking--

00:52:55.310 --> 00:52:57.810 align:middle line:84%
when you become parents, your
teachers will ask you to bring

00:52:57.810 --> 00:53:01.090 align:middle line:84%
in boxes of tissues and things
like that, and you'll be like,

00:53:01.090 --> 00:53:04.320 align:middle line:84%
what broken, failed country
do we live in where you cannot

00:53:04.320 --> 00:53:07.570 align:middle line:84%
simply buy all the tissues
that you need for the children?

00:53:07.570 --> 00:53:08.070 align:middle line:90%
Hm?

00:53:08.070 --> 00:53:08.830 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Or paper.

00:53:08.830 --> 00:53:09.125 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Paper.

00:53:09.125 --> 00:53:10.258 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Two reams.

00:53:10.258 --> 00:53:11.050 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:53:11.050 --> 00:53:11.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:53:11.550 --> 00:53:14.400 align:middle line:84%
Two reams of paper a semester
for all your students.

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:16.630 align:middle line:84%
So the teachers don't have--
in some cases, they do,

00:53:16.630 --> 00:53:17.610 align:middle line:84%
but in a lot of
cases [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:17.610 --> 00:53:18.880 align:middle line:90%
Principals can buy things.

00:53:18.880 --> 00:53:20.680 align:middle line:84%
Directors of instructional
technology can buy things.

00:53:20.680 --> 00:53:22.060 align:middle line:84%
Assistant superintendents
can buy things.

00:53:22.060 --> 00:53:23.352 align:middle line:90%
Superintendents can buy things.

00:53:23.352 --> 00:53:25.170 align:middle line:84%
School boards in some
places can directly

00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:27.060 align:middle line:84%
authorize kinds of things,
although that's probably more

00:53:27.060 --> 00:53:28.352 align:middle line:90%
rare, but they can push for it.

00:53:28.352 --> 00:53:32.080 align:middle line:84%
But the point is, across
the 13,000 school districts,

00:53:32.080 --> 00:53:33.760 align:middle line:84%
in 130,000 schools
in this country,

00:53:33.760 --> 00:53:36.540 align:middle line:84%
the answer to the question, who
can buy something is different

00:53:36.540 --> 00:53:38.640 align:middle line:90%
in all of those contexts.

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:42.990 align:middle line:84%
That makes starting an education
technology company very, very

00:53:42.990 --> 00:53:46.600 align:middle line:84%
difficult. When
you go somewhere,

00:53:46.600 --> 00:53:49.685 align:middle line:84%
you don't even know
who can buy the thing.

00:53:49.685 --> 00:53:51.560 align:middle line:84%
You don't even know who
you're selling it to.

00:53:51.560 --> 00:53:54.680 align:middle line:84%
Now, there are people who do
know who buys all these things.

00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:56.440 align:middle line:84%
The only people in
the United States

00:53:56.440 --> 00:53:58.750 align:middle line:84%
who know for every school
and every district who

00:53:58.750 --> 00:54:01.720 align:middle line:84%
has the power to buy stuff
are giant publishers.

00:54:01.720 --> 00:54:04.433 align:middle line:84%
Pearson, Houghton Mifflin-- what
was the company you work for?

00:54:04.433 --> 00:54:05.350 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: McGraw Hill.

00:54:05.350 --> 00:54:06.170 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: McGraw Hill.

00:54:06.170 --> 00:54:06.670 align:middle line:90%
Perfect.

00:54:06.670 --> 00:54:08.390 align:middle line:84%
They have an army
of salespeople.

00:54:08.390 --> 00:54:11.620 align:middle line:84%
This army of salespeople have
fanned out across the 13,000

00:54:11.620 --> 00:54:14.278 align:middle line:84%
school districts and once
a month, once a quarter,

00:54:14.278 --> 00:54:16.820 align:middle line:84%
all the people who I just listed
who can actually buy things,

00:54:16.820 --> 00:54:17.987 align:middle line:90%
they take them out to lunch.

00:54:17.987 --> 00:54:19.280 align:middle line:90%
They bring them free supplies.

00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:20.928 align:middle line:90%
They do all that stuff.

00:54:20.928 --> 00:54:22.970 align:middle line:84%
They probably don't have
a master list somewhere.

00:54:22.970 --> 00:54:24.100 align:middle line:84%
Instead, they have
all these salespeople

00:54:24.100 --> 00:54:25.930 align:middle line:84%
who know the answer
to these questions.

00:54:25.930 --> 00:54:28.420 align:middle line:84%
So if you're a Silicon Valley
startup and you're like,

00:54:28.420 --> 00:54:30.673 align:middle line:84%
oh, I've got this new
AI thing I'm building.

00:54:30.673 --> 00:54:31.340 align:middle line:90%
It's super cool.

00:54:31.340 --> 00:54:31.860 align:middle line:90%
Da, da, da.

00:54:31.860 --> 00:54:35.560 align:middle line:84%
You don't have this massive
network of sales connections

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:37.943 align:middle line:90%
to help you figure this out.

00:54:37.943 --> 00:54:39.610 align:middle line:84%
One of my favorite
illustrations of this

00:54:39.610 --> 00:54:42.762 align:middle line:84%
is at a bunch of education
technology conferences--

00:54:42.762 --> 00:54:44.470 align:middle line:84%
how many of you ever
been to a conference

00:54:44.470 --> 00:54:46.965 align:middle line:90%
where you have a badge, right?

00:54:46.965 --> 00:54:48.590 align:middle line:84%
And you can put
stickers on your badges

00:54:48.590 --> 00:54:49.680 align:middle line:90%
or other things like this.

00:54:49.680 --> 00:54:52.020 align:middle line:84%
Basically one of the stickers
you can put on your badge--

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:53.810 align:middle line:84%
you can put your pronouns,
you can put some other things

00:54:53.810 --> 00:54:54.310 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:54:54.310 --> 00:54:56.130 align:middle line:90%
You can put buyer.

00:54:56.130 --> 00:54:57.740 align:middle line:84%
You can basically
signal to people,

00:54:57.740 --> 00:54:59.615 align:middle line:84%
I actually have the
power to purchase things.

00:54:59.615 --> 00:55:03.670 align:middle line:90%


00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:05.970 align:middle line:84%
When you walk through
the exhibit hall,

00:55:05.970 --> 00:55:08.000 align:middle line:84%
they will pay more
attention to the people who

00:55:08.000 --> 00:55:09.500 align:middle line:84%
have the little
buyer things on them

00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:12.560 align:middle line:84%
than they will to critical
social science researcher

00:55:12.560 --> 00:55:15.870 align:middle line:84%
badge is put on your thing
or something like that.

00:55:15.870 --> 00:55:18.200 align:middle line:84%
So here are some things
that you should take away.

00:55:18.200 --> 00:55:19.920 align:middle line:84%
The systems are
incredibly complicated.

00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:22.140 align:middle line:84%
There are all kinds of
stakeholders in them.

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:24.570 align:middle line:84%
But they are they're
not so infinite,

00:55:24.570 --> 00:55:26.270 align:middle line:84%
they're not so
complex that we can't

00:55:26.270 --> 00:55:28.910 align:middle line:84%
make a chart that
manages and starts

00:55:28.910 --> 00:55:30.300 align:middle line:90%
to understand all these things.

00:55:30.300 --> 00:55:35.100 align:middle line:84%
But as we move forward
and as we look at more--

00:55:35.100 --> 00:55:37.558 align:middle line:84%
a thing that you can do in any
kind of education technology

00:55:37.558 --> 00:55:39.933 align:middle line:84%
problem, any educational
setting, and be like, all right,

00:55:39.933 --> 00:55:42.300 align:middle line:84%
who are going to be the
stakeholders for this problem,

00:55:42.300 --> 00:55:45.680 align:middle line:84%
this thing, and what are they
going to be their interests?

00:55:45.680 --> 00:55:48.910 align:middle line:84%
And again, not just
their official interests,

00:55:48.910 --> 00:55:50.550 align:middle line:84%
their official state
policy interests

00:55:50.550 --> 00:55:52.800 align:middle line:84%
is to raise test scores
or something like that.

00:55:52.800 --> 00:55:55.470 align:middle line:84%
But one of my favorite
examples we're talking about

00:55:55.470 --> 00:55:59.460 align:middle line:84%
is televisions were more likely
to be used at the end of the day

00:55:59.460 --> 00:56:01.200 align:middle line:90%
because people were tired.

00:56:01.200 --> 00:56:04.110 align:middle line:84%
Like, your body's
circadian rhythm

00:56:04.110 --> 00:56:06.870 align:middle line:84%
indicates how technologies
be used in classroom.

00:56:06.870 --> 00:56:08.650 align:middle line:90%
That is just as real.

00:56:08.650 --> 00:56:10.980 align:middle line:84%
It is just as real
as the policy mandate

00:56:10.980 --> 00:56:14.460 align:middle line:84%
to raise test scores
or the school board's

00:56:14.460 --> 00:56:17.370 align:middle line:84%
enthusiasm for technology
or whatever it is.

00:56:17.370 --> 00:56:20.610 align:middle line:84%
And so in any kind of
system, we can figure out

00:56:20.610 --> 00:56:22.470 align:middle line:84%
who are the stakeholders
in the system, what

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:23.880 align:middle line:84%
are the interests of
those things, what

00:56:23.880 --> 00:56:24.850 align:middle line:90%
are the disinterests.

00:56:24.850 --> 00:56:27.540 align:middle line:84%
A thing is more likely
to work if it attends

00:56:27.540 --> 00:56:31.180 align:middle line:84%
to those real, meaningful,
felt interests,

00:56:31.180 --> 00:56:34.360 align:middle line:84%
and if it avoids those real,
meaningful, felt disinterests.

00:56:34.360 --> 00:56:37.110 align:middle line:84%
And of course, we're doing
this in a very detailed way

00:56:37.110 --> 00:56:38.050 align:middle line:90%
in education.

00:56:38.050 --> 00:56:39.840 align:middle line:84%
But if you were
to leave education

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:41.680 align:middle line:84%
and you were to go
into health care,

00:56:41.680 --> 00:56:43.510 align:middle line:84%
if you were to go
into legal technology,

00:56:43.510 --> 00:56:46.415 align:middle line:84%
if you were going to retail, you
can do the exact same exercise.

00:56:46.415 --> 00:56:48.540 align:middle line:84%
The technology that you're
building for a hospital,

00:56:48.540 --> 00:56:49.998 align:middle line:84%
it has to work for
a doctor, but it

00:56:49.998 --> 00:56:52.290 align:middle line:84%
has to work for a nurse and
an orderly and a patient

00:56:52.290 --> 00:56:54.630 align:middle line:84%
and the patient's representative
and the hospital's

00:56:54.630 --> 00:56:57.420 align:middle line:84%
lawyers and all these
different kinds of people.

00:56:57.420 --> 00:57:00.000 align:middle line:84%
And so on the one hand,
especially when people

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:03.510 align:middle line:84%
are designers of software, we
can have the sense of, oh, I'm

00:57:03.510 --> 00:57:04.780 align:middle line:90%
building things for a user.

00:57:04.780 --> 00:57:06.863 align:middle line:84%
I'm building things for
this individual user who's

00:57:06.863 --> 00:57:08.947 align:middle line:84%
got their fingers on their
keyboard or their thumb

00:57:08.947 --> 00:57:10.500 align:middle line:84%
on the screen or
something like that.

00:57:10.500 --> 00:57:11.760 align:middle line:84%
But I would encourage
you, as we're

00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:13.177 align:middle line:84%
thinking through
all these things,

00:57:13.177 --> 00:57:15.960 align:middle line:84%
to think about these networks
of interests and stakeholders

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:17.220 align:middle line:90%
much more broadly.

00:57:17.220 --> 00:57:18.420 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:18.420 --> 00:57:21.550 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk to some education
school deans on Wednesday.

00:57:21.550 --> 00:57:23.835 align:middle line:84%
We'll learn a little bit
about implementation,

00:57:23.835 --> 00:57:25.960 align:middle line:84%
how you overcome some of
these kinds of challenges.

00:57:25.960 --> 00:57:28.502 align:middle line:84%
From me, we'll hear a little
bit about the National Education

00:57:28.502 --> 00:57:31.530 align:middle line:84%
Technology plan, and I will
pair you up with each other

00:57:31.530 --> 00:57:35.087 align:middle line:84%
to be able to do some peer
editing of your essays.

00:57:35.087 --> 00:57:36.670 align:middle line:84%
And that will get
us through the week.

00:57:36.670 --> 00:57:37.470 align:middle line:90%
Sound good?

00:57:37.470 --> 00:57:38.130 align:middle line:90%
Bueno.

00:57:38.130 --> 00:57:39.963 align:middle line:84%
Let me know if I can
help you with anything.

00:57:39.963 --> 00:57:42.770 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, have a wonderful
rest of your afternoon.

00:57:42.770 --> 00:57:45.000 align:middle line:90%